 596:   equality is the direct negative of the breach.
 597:   **Why no disposable stand-in plays the personal stack's part:** docker object names derive
 598:   from the project id, so a stand-in carrying the personal project id IS the personal stack's
 599:   container set â€” creating or removing it would itself touch what must stay untouched â€” and a
 600:   stand-in under any other id is just another bystander, a role slot-1 already fills. A
 601:   stand-in adds cost and no proof.
 602:   **Why the proof does not depend on the personal stack's run state:** the CLI selects the
 603:   docker objects it acts on by resolved project identity, not by what is running â€” the
 604:   incident demonstrated this by destroying the personal identity's containers while running
 605:   slot-2 went untouched. A leak under the amended helper surfaces as one of: the identity
 606:   pre-read reporting the wrong target (refusal before the reset), the reset acting on the
 607:   personal identity (snapshot mismatch), or the reset failing to act on slot-2 (the canary
 608:   survives and the done-criterion fails). No leak shape produces a green transcript.
 609:   **The hostile condition is mandatory:** the spike parent deliberately carries the `.env`
 610:   override that caused the breach, so a pass proves the helper closes the route WHILE the
 611:   route is loaded â€” not that the route happened to be empty on the day.
 612: - **E2 amended: UNBLOCKED (founder, 2026-08-10).** S3 as amended runs now; S5 follows only
 613:   after S3's done-criterion is met, per the plan's build-order rule. The no-touch rule on the
 614:   personal stack is unchanged and permanent.
