 662: ### Terra's findings
 663: 
 664: **[T1] ACCEPT â€” the critical.** Claim: "An empty or partially written lock file is treated as a
 665: dead holder under `dead-pid-only`, so a second occupier can delete a live process's just-created
 666: claim."
 667: Verified at runner.ts:440: `readHolder` returns `{}` for an unreadable or half-written file, and
 668: `holderIsLive({})` is false under every policy â€” so an unidentifiable holder is takeover-eligible
 669: even under the policy whose ruled meaning is "a DEAD HOLDER PID and nothing else". The window is
 670: the microseconds between `openSync(file, 'wx')` and the `writeSync`, and it is real. Fix, scoped
 671: to `dead-pid-only`: an unidentifiable holder is NEVER takeover-eligible. After one bounded
 672: re-read (a short pause, then read again â€” enough to skate over the write window), a holder that
 673: still has no parseable pid refuses loudly, naming the file and the manual deletion path. The
 674: same guard applies to the re-read INSIDE the takeover gate, so the gate cannot remove an
 675: unidentifiable file either. `stale-or-dead` keeps today's behaviour: that path predates this
 676: item and is another item's business if anyone wants it changed.
