le was present in the parent process\\n80:   PASS  the identity pre-read named slot 2 before the reset\\n81:   PASS  the reset ran through the helper and exited zero\\n82:   PASS  the slot 2 canary is GONE â€” the reset acted on slot 2\\n83:   PASS  the slot 1 canary SURVIVES â€” the reset acted on nothing else\\n84:   PASS  the personal docker snapshots are equal on every identity field\\n85: \\n86: THE SPIKE PASSED: the wall holds under the exact condition that broke it.\\n87: \\n88: Residue: slot 1 keeps a public.spike_canary table until its next occupancy, which resets it.\\n89: END OF S3 TRANSCRIPT (re-proof)\\n90: \\n91: ================================================================================\\n92: POSTSCRIPT, written by hand after the run.\\n93: ================================================================================\\n94: \\n95: THE TREE THE RUN USED. The head line above says 3ca44eb, which was the last\\n96: commit at the moment of the run. The working tree also carried one uncommitted\\n97: edit: writeCanary drops and recreates its table instead of using CREATE TABLE IF\\n98: NOT EXISTS. Both are in the commit that carries this transcript.\\n99: \\n100: THE FIRST ATTEMPT, recorded because a transcript that hides one is not evidence.\\n101: The spike was run once before this run and stopped at step (c) with:\\n102: \\n103:   null value in column \\\"id\\\" of relation \\\"spike_canary\\\" violates not-null constraint\\n104: \\n105: Slot 1 still held a spike_canary table left by the first, failed spike of\\n106: 2026-08-09, with a column set of its own. CREATE TABLE IF NOT EXISTS therefore\\n107: created nothing and the insert failed on that table's constraint. The stop was a\\n108: canary client problem in a slot database. No identity was resolved, no CLI\\n109: command ran after the preflight reads, and step (h) of that attempt already\\n110: reported the personal docker snapshot IDENTICAL. The fix was to drop and\\n111: recreate the canary