ty pre-read named slot 2 before the reset\\n81:   PASS  the reset ran through the helper and exited zero\\n82:   PASS  the slot 2 canary is GONE — the reset acted on slot 2\\n83:   PASS  the slot 1 canary SURVIVES — the reset acted on nothing else\\n84:   PASS  the personal docker snapshots are equal on every identity field\\n85: \\n86: THE SPIKE PASSED: the wall holds under the exact condition that broke it.\\n87: \\n88: Residue: slot 1 keeps a public.spike_canary table until its next occupancy, which resets it.\\n89: END OF S3 TRANSCRIPT (re-proof)\\n90: \\n91: ================================================================================\\n92: POSTSCRIPT, written by hand after the run.\\n93: ================================================================================\\n94: \\n95: THE TREE THE RUN USED. The head line above says 3ca44eb, which was the last\\n96: commit at the moment of the run. The working tree also carried one uncommitted\\n97: edit: writeCanary drops and recreates its table instead of using CREATE TABLE IF\\n98: NOT EXISTS. Both are in the commit that carries this transcript.\\n99: \\n100: THE FIRST ATTEMPT, recorded because a transcript that hides one is not evidence.\\n101: The spike was run once before this run and stopped at step (c) with:\\n102: \\n103:   null value in column \\\"id\\\" of relation \\\"spike_canary\\\" violates not-null constraint\\n104: \\n105: Slot 1 still held a spike_canary table left by the first, failed spike of\\n106: 2026-08-09, with a column set of its own. CREATE TABLE IF NOT EXISTS therefore\\n107: created nothing and the insert failed on that table's constraint. The stop was a\\n108: canary client problem in a slot database. No identity was resolved, no CLI\\n109: command ran after the preflight reads, and step (h) of that attempt already\\n110: reported the personal docker snapshot IDENTICAL. The fix was to drop and\\n111: recreate the canary table, and the run above is the re-run.\\n112: \\n113: TWO INDEPENDENT READS AGREE THAT THE PERSONAL STACK WAS NOT TOUCHED. Step (h)\\n114: compares the docker identity snapshot and reports it identical. A separate\\n115: docker ps taken after the run reports every personal container still up with the\\n116: same uptime it had before the run, so nothing recreated or restarted one. The\\n117: tracked .env is byte for byte unchanged, verified by SHA-256 before and after.\\n118: \\n119: END OF POSTSCRIPT\\n\\n(End of file - total 119 lines)\\n</content>\",\"metadata\":{\"preview\":\"\\nAI4DEV-79 (parallel DB slot pool) - step S3 as amended by ruling E5, the isolation re-proof.\\n\\nREDACTION: database passwords and any key-shaped token are replaced before printing.\\nContainer names, project ids and ports are kept: they are the evidence.\\n\\ndate      : 2026-08-10T06:50:54.123Z\\nmachine   : MyPC\\nworktree  : C:/Users/nirdr/Downloads/ai4good/.claude/worktrees/agent-a064eb77d7bb37dd7/\\nbranch    : nirdrang/ai4dev-79-a-pool-of-local-database-slots-so-items-verify-in-parallel\\nhead      : 3ca44eb6cddd15c9a13d289d1c6ec9ea64a526af\\npool root : C:\\\\Users\\\\nirdr\\\\AppData\\\\Local\\\\ai4good-build\\\\db-slots\\n\\n=== the hostile condition, mandatory ===\\nSUPABASE_PROJECT_ID IS PRESENT IN THIS PROCESS: \\\"poancmeitlmxejofwzuu\\\".\\nThat is the founder's personal project id, and it is the exact override that destroyed the personal\\ndatabase on 2026-08-09. It stays in this process for the whole run. Every CLI call below is made\\nthrough the shared helper, which states the slot identity positively in the child environment.\\n\\n=== (a) BEFORE: docker identity snapshot of the personal stack (a READ; nothing is written) ===\",\"truncated\":false,\"loaded\":[],\"display\":{\"type\":\"file\",\"path\":\"C:\\\\Users\\\\nirdr\\\\Downloads\\\\ai4good\\\\.claude\\\\worktrees\\\\agent-a064eb77d7bb37dd7\\\\loop\\\\items\\\\AI4DEV-79\\\\spike-isolation-2.txt\",\"text\":\"\\nAI4DEV-79 (parallel DB slot pool) - step S3 as amended by ruling 