\\\"timestamp\\\":1786350169308,\\\"sessionID\\\":\\\"ses_01541a198ffeMeZOHEAs76N82N\\\",\\\"part\\\":{\\\"type\\\":\\\"tool\\\",\\\"tool\\\":\\\"read\\\",\\\"callID\\\":\\\"call_00_JvTg7MsHz9ojj3oDB5CU0713\\\",\\\"state\\\":{\\\"status\\\":\\\"completed\\\",\\\"input\\\":{\\\"filePath\\\":\\\"C:\\\\\\\\Users\\\\\\\\nirdr\\\\\\\\Downloads\\\\\\\\ai4good\\\\\\\\.claude\\\\\\\\worktrees\\\\\\\\agent-a064eb77d7bb37dd7\\\\\\\\loop\\\\\\\\items\\\\\\\\AI4DEV-79\\\\\\\\artifacts\\\\\\\\gate1-sol-distillate.md\\\",\\\"limit\\\":40},\\\"output\\\":\\\"<path>C:\\\\\\\\Users\\\\\\\\nirdr\\\\\\\\Downloads\\\\\\\\ai4good\\\\\\\\.claude\\\\\\\\worktrees\\\\\\\\agent-a064eb77d7bb37dd7\\\\\\\\loop\\\\\\\\items\\\\\\\\AI4DEV-79\\\\\\\\artifacts\\\\\\\\gate1-sol-distillate.md</path>\\\\n<type>file</type>\\\\n<content>\\\\n1: SOURCE   loop/items/AI4DEV-79/artifacts/gate1-sol-output.md\\\\n2: REVIEWER gpt-5.6-sol, reasoning effort xhigh, sandbox read-only (codex CLI 0.146.0, session id 019fe865-7f42-76d0-a39c-fbb5221a7f75)\\\\n3: COUNT    15 findings in source → 15 extracted\\\\n4: NOTES    Declared count line \\\\\\\"PLAN REVIEW: 15 FINDINGS\\\\\\\" matches the extracted count. Severity scale as stated by the reviewer: high = isolation/data-safety violation or a false completion gate; medium = supported states or required proof are missing; low = a factual error that weakens evidence.\\\\n5: \\\\n6: [1] severity: high   loop/items/AI4DEV-79/plan.md:106\\\\n7:     claim: \\\\\\\"File equality cannot enforce D3's invariant that the on-disk config describes the successfully restarted stack.\\\\\\\"\\\\n8:     why it matters: \\\\\\\"If the process stops after writing config but before a successful restart, the next occupancy sees equal text and skips restart, leaving either a stopped slot or a running stack with the previous auth behavior.\\\\\\\"\\\\n9:     unverified-runtime-claim: no\\\\n10:     raw: gate1-sol-output.md:3-6\\\\n11: \\\\n12: [2] severity: high   loop/items/AI4DEV-79/plan.md:120\\\\n13:     claim: \\\\\\\"D4 and D6 create separate reservation and occupancy files instead of the ruled single per-slot claim transitioning between reserved and occupied states.\\\\\\\"\\\\n14:     why it matters: \\\\\\\"A sweep can delete the reservation while its occupancy lock remains, allowing another item to reserve t...\",\"metadata\":{\"matches\":9,\"truncated\":false},\"title\":\"CODE REVIEW: \\\\d+ FINDINGS|PLAN REVIEW: \\\\d+ FINDINGS\",\"time\":{\"start\":1786350195248,\"end\":1786350195299}},\"id\":\"prt_feac4d0a0001bmK9GAHNJYGl3K\",\"sessionID\":\"ses_01541a198ffeMeZOHEAs76N82N\",\"messageID\":\"msg_feac471fe001AZqsS1ZDeoeVoy\"}}\r\n+{\"type\":\"text\",\"timestamp\":1786350195662,\"sessionID\":\"ses_01541a198ffeMeZOHEAs76N82N\",\"part\":{\"id\":\"prt_feac4cfb2001i0ocjU9XgInG20\",\"messageID\":\"msg_feac471fe001AZqsS1ZDeoeVoy\",\"sessionID\":\"ses_01541a198ffeMeZOHEAs76N82N\",\"type\":\"text\",\"text\":\"Let me confirm the raw gate-2 outputs' count lines and scan the whole item record for any integration-tier green claim.\",\"time\":{\"start\":1786350194611,\"end\":1786350195660}}}\r\n+{\"type\":\"tool_use\",\"timestamp\":1786350195882,\"sessionID\":\"ses_01541a198ffeMeZOHEAs76N82N\",\"part\":{\"type\":\"tool\",\"tool\":\"grep\",\"callID\":\"call_01_YVonSjtqbgg65lguuUoq9341\",\"state\":{\"status\":\"error\",\"input\":{\"path\":\"C:\\\\Users\\\\nirdr\\\\Downloads\\\\ai4good\\\\.claude\\\\worktrees\\\\agent-a064eb77d7bb37dd7\\\\loop\\\\items\\\\AI4DEV-79\",\"pattern\":\"integration.*green|green.*integration\"},\"error\":\"Ripgrep JSON record exceeded 65536 bytes\",\"time\":{\"start\":1786350195586,\"end\":1786350195878}},\"id\":\"prt_feac4d2690016DCqsO1AJiAEcw\",\"sessionID\":\"ses_01541a198ffeMeZOHEAs76N82N\",\"messageID\":\"msg_feac471fe001AZqsS1ZDeoeVoy\"}}\r\n",
loop/items/AI4DEV-79/artifacts/audit-luna-output.stderr.log:11448:C:\Users\nirdr\Downloads\ai4good\.claude\worktrees\agent-a064eb77d7bb37dd7\loop\items\AI4DEV-79\artifacts\audit-flash-identity.raw.json:150: "output": "<path>C:\\Users\\nirdr\\Downloads\\ai4good\\.claude\\worktrees\\agent-a064eb77d7bb37dd7\\loop\\items\\AI4DEV-79\\plan.md</path>\n<type>file</type>\n<content>\n1: # Plan — AI4DEV-79 (parallel local DB slot pool)\n2: \n3: Written by the plan sitting (orchestrator, fable @ xhigh), 2026-08-10. Chain, derived from the\n4: branch: AI4DEV-79 (parallel DB slot pool) → AI4DEV-3 (AT harness), root label `attr:bringup`.\n5: Foundation work: no requirement above it, no acceptance-test ids of its own.\n6: \n7: ## 1. The ruled design — quoted from the board item, because the board is not in the tree\n8: \n9: The Linear description carries the founder-ruled design (ruled 2026-08-09). This gate critiques\n10: the mechanism below it; it does not reopen these decisions. The ruled points, quoted:\n11: \n12: > * **A pool of 2 standing Supabase stacks** (\"slots\"), each with its own project id and port\n13: >   block. Concurrency = pool size. The default stack on ports 54321+ is the founder's personal\n14: >   stack and stays OUTSIDE the pool, untouchable.\n15: > * **Warm slots**: the stacks stay running. (Flipping to start-on-acquire later is a one-line\n16: >   change.)\n17: > * **One claim file per slot, two states, three owners**: the coordinator RESERVES a slot when\n18: >   an item starts (`/work` time — admission control); the harness runner OCCUPIES it for each\n19: >   verify window (atomic create-new claim, pid stamped, release in a `finally`); the\n20: >   coordinator RELEASES the reservation at the item sweep.\n21: > * **Full pool = the next database-needing item is REJECTED at start.** No flagged limbo.\n22: >   Items that need no database (docs, contracts) skip the reservation, stated at start.\n23: > * **State is never inherited.** First act of every occupancy: copy the item tree's own\n24: >   `supabase/migrations` + seed into the slot directory and run `supabase db reset --workdir\n25: >   <slot>`. The previous holder is never trusted, including a crashed one.\n26: > * **Stale claims are broken LOUDLY** — dead holder pid, takeover recorded in the claim, never\n27: >   silent.\n28: > * **The evidence names the slot**: the verify transcript carries one line — slot, reset\n29: >   performed, migration state. A green that cannot name its reset ran against unknown state.\n30: > * **Identity/data split**: each slot directory permanently owns its `config.toml` (project id\n31: >   + ports = identity); the item supplies migrations by copy (data). The repo's committed\n32: >   `supabase/config.toml` is never edited, so no item diff is ever polluted.\n33: \n34: What gets built, per the item: (1) a one-time setup script; (2) `tests/at/harness/db-pool.ts`\n35: (~150–250 lines) exporting `readPool / occupy / prepare / stackEnv / release / evidence`;\n36: (3) one hook in `tests/at/harness/runner.ts` gated behind the integration tier, loop-tier\n37: behavior byte-identical; (4) reservation helpers (PowerShell, work library) plus reservation\n38: lines in the /work skill, the conductor's spawn facts, and release in the coordinator sweep.\n39: \n40: The tests, per the item: `db-pool.selftest.ts` with named tests (two concurrent occupies on one\n41: slot → exactly one wins; dead-pid claim → loud takeover, recorded; release fires from `finally`\n42: even when the suite throws; the evidence line carries slot + migration state), and:\n43: \n44: > **The isolation spike comes FIRST and is a done-criterion**: canary rows in slot-1 AND in the\n45: > personal stack must survive a `db reset` aimed at slot-2. Never assume the wall — prove it\n46: > before building on it.\n47: \n48: > Suites change ZERO lines — they already reach the stack only through the env the runner\n49: > injects.\n50: \n51: ## 2. Facts about the tree this plan stands on (verified this sitting; pointers, not paste)\n52: \n53: - F1. The runner's integration-tier sequence is `tests/at/harness/runner.ts` main(), the\n54:   `tier !== 'loop'` block (~lines 1044–1112): read `supabase/config.toml` at REPO_ROOT →\n55:   machine-wide stack lock → `supabase status` → prove-local checks → readiness → `db reset\n56:   --local` → readiness → prove migrations replayed → inject `AT_SUPABASE_URL`,\n57:   `AT_SUPABASE_DB_URL`, `AT_SUPABASE_ANON_KEY`, `AT_SUPABASE_SERVICE_ROLE_KEY` into the vitest\n58:   child. Suites see only that env (runner.ts ~1108–1111). The item's \"suites change ZERO lines\"\n59:   claim is true against the tree.\n60: - F2. The tiers are `loop | integration | drill` (runner.ts:47). The `tier !== 'loop'` guard\n61:   means the DRILL tier also reaches the repo-config stack today.\n62: - F3. `acquireStackLock` (runner.ts ~343–442) is already an atomic create-new claim file, pid\n63:   and start-time stamped, with a race-proven stale-takeover gate (runner.selftest.ts) and a\n64:   loud console line on takeover. Its release runs inside the runner's `finally` chain\n65:   (`cleanupRun`, runner.ts ~938–946, called from main's `finally`). The lock file lives at\n66:   `<LOCALAPPDATA|XDG_CACHE_HOME|tmpdir>/ai4good-build/at-locks/at-verify-<projectId>-<apiPort>.lock`.\n67: - F4. The personal stack's identity (`supabase/config.toml`): `project_id\n68:   \"poancmeitlmxejofwzuu\"`; ports 54321 (api), 54322 (db), 54320 (shadow), 54323 (studio),\n69:   54324 (local_smtp), 54327 (analytics), 54329 (pooler, disabled), and `edge_runtime`\n70:   `inspector_port` 8083. Seed config `[db.seed] sql_paths = [\"./seed.sql\"]` — and\n71:   `supabase/seed.sql` does NOT exist in the tree today.\n72: - F5. `supabase/functions/` carries two live edge functions plus `_shared` modules, and\n73:   `config.toml` pins `verify_jwt = true` for both. A slot that lacks the functions directory\n74:   serves 404 where the personal stack serves the function.\n75: - F6. `config.toml` uses `env()` substitution for OAuth credentials; a missed env resolves to a\n76:   literal string and does not stop the stack (documented in config.toml ~line 410, measured on\n77:   the sign-in item).\n78: - F7. CI (`.github/workflows/ci.yml`, required check `verify`) runs on `ubuntu-latest`, or on\n79:   a self-hosted runner when the repository variable `CI_RUNNER_LABEL` is set (ci.yml:44;\n80:   corrected by gate-1 [15]), with NO Docker step either way: typecheck, `at:selftest` (vitest\n81:   over `tests/at/harness/`), `at:check` per suite, `at:verify --tier loop --expect` per\n82:   declaration. Every test this item adds to `harness/` must be green with no live stack, no\n83:   Docker, and no Windows-only path assumption.\n84: - F8. The Supabase CLI is pinned in devDependencies (`supabase ^2.110.0`) and always invoked as\n85:   `bun --no-env-file <INSTALL_ROOT>/node_modules/supabase/dist/supabase.js` (runner.ts:69,\n86:   460–463). The CLI takes a global `--workdir` flag naming a directory that contains a\n87:   `supabase/` project folder — used by nothing in this repo yet, so it is UNVERIFIED here;\n88:   step S2's done-criterion proves it before anything depends on it.\n89: - F9. The work library pattern for locks is `Acquire-WorkLock` / `Release-WorkLock`\n90:   (`loop/work/work-lib.ps1` ~196–223). The /work skill's claim step and spawn-facts step are\n91:   SKILL.md steps 4–6 (~lines 91–101); the coordinator sweep step is SKILL.md ~line 170.\n92: - F10. The verify surface for this item (foundation work, no AT ids): `bun run typecheck`,\n93:   `bun run at:selftest`, `bun run at:check req-001`, `bun run at:verify req-001 --tier loop\n94:   --expect` — the same four things CI runs, and all four are green on main today.\n95: \n96: ## 3. Decisions — the mechanism under the ruled design\n97: \n98: - **D1. Pool root.** `<LOCALAPPDATA ?? XDG_CACHE_HOME ?? tmpdir>/ai4good-build/db-slots/`,\n99:   the same base-resolution rule as the runner's `lockDir()` (F3), so the pool lives beside the\n100:   locks it cooperates with, outside every worktree. Layout: `slot-1/supabase/…`,\n101:   `slot-2/supabase/…`, `reservations/slot-N.json`. Env override `AT_DB_POOL_ROOT` exists for\n102:   the selftests only, exactly as `AT_REPO_ROOT` exists for the runner's own tests.\n103: - **D2. Slot identity.** Slot N owns `project_id \"ai4good-slot-N\"` and the port overlay\n104:   `54xxx + N*1000`: slot-1 = 55320/55321/55322/55323/55324/55327/55329, inspector 8093;\n105:   slot-2 = 56320/56321/56322/56323/56324/56327/56329, inspector 8103. Distinct project ids\n106:   give distinct Docker container and volume names; distinct port blocks give the wall the\n107:   spike proves. No slot value may fall in 54320–54329 or be 8083 (D5 enforces).\n108:   The overlay rule is GENERAL (gate-1 [5]): every active key named `port` or ending `_port`,\n109:   in every section, with a value in 54000–54999, maps to value + N*1000.\n110:   `edge_runtime.inspector_port` maps 8083 → 8083 + N*10. Any other active port value refuses\n111:   loudly as unmappable — a human decides, not a guess.\n112: - **D3. Slot config regeneration — identity is permanent, everything else is data.** The ruled\n113:   identity/data split names project id + ports as the slot's identity. Auth flags, seed paths\n114:   and `verify_jwt` pins also live in `config.toml`, and items change them (F4's\n115:   `enable_confirmations` was flipped by an item). A slot config frozen at setup time would\n116:   drift from the item tree's config, and a verify on a slot would grade different auth\n117:   behavior than a verify on the stack the config describes. So `prepare()` regenerates the\n118:   slot's `config.toml` on every occupancy: take the item tree's `supabase/config.toml`\n119:   verbatim, overlay ONLY the identity fields (project_id, the seven port keys, inspector_port)\n120:   with the slot's permanent values, using the same section-aware line scan `readLocalConfig`\n121:   already uses, and the general port rule in D2. The keep-warm decision compares against a\n122:   MARKER, not the file (gate-1 [1]): `slot-N/.last-start.json` holds the hash of the config the\n123:   stack last STARTED with, written only after a successful start. Regenerated hash equals the\n124:   marker → nothing happens, the warm stack keeps running (the ruled default). Different or\n125:   absent marker → prepare writes the config, restarts that slot's stack (`stop` + `start\n126:   --workdir`) before the reset — the auth container reads config at start, not at reset — and\n127:   writes the marker only when the restart succeeds. A crash between the write and the restart\n128:   therefore forces a restart on the next occupancy instead of hiding behind equal text.\n129: - **D4. Occupancy = the existing stack lock, extended.** `occupy()` acquires\n130:   `acquireStackLock(slotConfig, requirement)` (F3) — it is already the atomic create-new,\n131:   pid-stamped, stale-takeover-with-loud-line claim the ruled text describes, and it is already\n132:   released in the runner's `finally` chain. Two extensions. First: when a stale claim is\n133:   broken, the new claim file records `tookOverFrom` (the dead holder's pid and start time), so\n134:   the takeover is recorded IN the claim, not only on the console. Second (gate-1 [3]):\n135:   `acquireStackLock` gains a takeover-policy parameter; the pool passes dead-pid-only, so a\n136:   LIVE holder is never taken over at any age — occupy refuses loudly and names the holder.\n137:   Existing call sites keep today's default policy unchanged.\n138: - **D5. The personal block is refused in code, not by convention.** db-pool refuses to occupy,\n139:   prepare, reset or emit env for any slot whose config carries the repo config's `project_id`,\n140:   any port in 54320–54329, or inspector port 8083. The scan covers EVERY active port-valued\n141:   key in the config, not a fixed list (gate-1 [5]). The check runs before anything\n142:   destructive, every time, and has its own named selftest. This is the \"untouchable\" ruling as\n143:   an executable guard.\n144: - **D6. Reservation lookup is derived, never declared.** The coordinator's `Reserve-DbSlot`\n145:   writes `reservations/slot-N.json` (exclusive create; content: item id, branch, timestamp,\n146:   holder). At verify time the runner derives its item id from the current branch (the house\n147:   attribution rule) and occupies the slot whose reservation names that item. A run with no\n148:   reservation for its item refuses as INFRASTRUCTURE, naming `Reserve-DbSlot` — no silent\n149:   fallback onto any free slot, because admission control is the coordinator's, not the\n150:   runner's. `AT_DB_SLOT=N` is the explicit override for runs outside an item (the founder, the\n151:   evidence gate, the spike); the occupancy claim still applies.\n152:   Three hardenings (gate-1 [2], [12]): the branch parser is a pure function over the branch\n153:   string and FAILS CLOSED — exactly one item id, or it refuses naming the condition (zero ids,\n154:   several ids, detached HEAD, git call failed); `occupy` re-reads the reservation AFTER the\n155:   claim is acquired and releases-then-refuses if it no longer names the runner's item;\n156:   `Release-DbSlot` deletes a reservation only when it names the item being swept, and refuses\n157:   loudly while a live occupancy claim exists on that slot.\n158: - **D7. Data copy at occupancy.** Before the reset, prepare deletes and re-copies into the\n159:   slot: `supabase/migrations/*.sql` (timestamped files only, the runner's own pattern),\n160:   `supabase/seed.sql` when the item tree has one — and removes the slot's copy when it does\n161:   not (F4) — and `supabase/functions/**` (F5: integration suites can call edge functions\n162:   through the injected URL). The previous holder's files are never trusted; delete-then-copy,\n163:   not merge.\n164:   Fail closed on the closure (gate-1 [6]): after regeneration, prepare scans the config for\n165:   active relative-path values (seed `sql_paths`, `schema_paths`, email template files, TLS and\n166:   signing key paths) and refuses loudly on any path it cannot copy from the item tree — no\n167:   stack starts half-provisioned. Env policy, decided: slot start and restart inherit the\n168:   invoking process env; a missed `env()` resolves to a literal string and does not stop the\n169:   stack (F6, measured). [The inherit-env sentence is SUPERSEDED by ruling E1's strip — see §9,\n170:   flash note 1: every `env()` substitution resolves to the literal string on a slot, always.]\n171: - **D8. The runner hook is integration-tier only.** In main's stack block: when\n172:   `tier === 'integration'`, resolve the stack through the pool — occupy → prepare (copy,\n173:   config, reset via `--workdir`, readiness, prove migrations) → the same prove-local checks\n174:   against the SLOT's config → inject the slot's env — and print the evidence line. The loop\n175:   tier stays byte-identical (it never enters the block). The DRILL tier REFUSES as\n176:   INFRASTRUCTURE (gate-1 [4]): \"the drill tier's stack is not yet decided\" — it no longer\n177:   reaches any stack, personal or slot. Nothing in the tree invokes `--tier drill` today\n178:   (verified: no script, no CI step, no process file), so nothing breaks, and the last harness\n179:   path that could reset the personal stack is closed in code. The follow-up item that decides\n180:   drill's stack replaces the refusal.\n181: - **D9. Existing helpers are parameterized, not duplicated.** `readLocalConfig`,\n182:   `readStackStatus`, `resetLocalDatabase` and `proveMigrationsReplayed` currently hard-code\n183:   REPO_ROOT (F1). Each gains a workdir/config parameter defaulting to today's value —\n184:   loop-tier call-site behavior is unchanged — and the pool path passes the slot.\n185:   No second copy of the status parser, the prove-local checks or the reset process handling.\n186:   `lockDir()` honors an `AT_LOCK_DIR` env override (gate-1 [13]), the same pattern as\n187:   `AT_REPO_ROOT`; the default is unchanged; the db-pool selftests set it so their claims never\n188:   touch the machine-wide lock directory.\n189: - **D10. One place builds configs.** All TOML reading and generation lives in db-pool.ts\n190:   (TypeScript). The one-time setup is a CLI entry in the same file (`bun\n191:   tests/at/harness/db-pool.ts setup`, the `import.meta.main` pattern runner.ts uses): create\n192:   both slot directories, generate both configs from the current tree + identity overlay, start\n193:   both stacks via the pinned CLI, print both status reports. The PowerShell helpers\n194:   (`loop/work/db-slots.ps1`: `Reserve-DbSlot`, `Release-DbSlot`, `Get-DbSlotPool`) do file\n195:   operations only — reserve, release, list — mirroring the `Acquire-WorkLock` house pattern\n196:   (F9). No TOML logic in PowerShell, so the overlay cannot fork.\n197: - **D11. The evidence line.** One line on the verify transcript, printed by the runner on the\n198:   pool path: `at:verify — db slot <N> (<project_id>, api <port>) — reset OK — migrations: <E>\n199:   expected, <A> applied`. Slot, reset performed, migration state — the ruled three.\n200: - **D12. No slicing.** The diff is one mechanism — pool module, its selftests, one runner\n201:   hook, small PowerShell helpers, and process-file lines. The parts reference each other; a\n202:   per-slice code gate would review the claim logic apart from the tests that pin it. One\n203:   draft-code gate reads the whole diff.\n204: - **D13. One shared invocation helper (ruling E1, §8; added by the resumed draft sitting,\n205:   2026-08-10).** Every slot CLI invocation goes through one helper in db-pool.ts. The helper:\n206:   sets `SUPABASE_PROJECT_ID=<slot project id>` positively in the child env; strips every other\n207:   `SUPABASE_*` variable; invokes `bun --no-env-file` per the house rule; sets the child working\n208:   directory to the slot. The destructive path (reset, stop) performs an identity read through\n209:   the same helper first — the status the CLI reports must carry the slot's ports, and any\n210:   project identity it reports must be the slot's — and refuses loudly on mismatch. The read is\n211:   structurally ON the destructive path, never a separate call a caller can skip. This applies\n212:   to every slot-aimed use of the parameterized runner helpers (D9) too.\n213: \n214: ## 4. Steps, each with its done-criterion\n215: \n216: Build order note: the ruled text says the spike comes FIRST — before anything is built ON the\n217: wall. The spike needs the slots to exist, so S1–S2 create them, S3 proves the wall, and only\n218: then S5 builds the runner hook on top of it.\n219: \n220: - **S1. `tests/at/harness/db-pool.ts`** — pool root resolution (D1), config read/overlay/\n221:   generation (D2, D3), `readPool`, `occupy` (D4), `prepare` (D3, D7), `stackEnv`, `release`,\n222:   `evidence` (D11), the personal-block guard (D5), reservation lookup (D6), and the `setup`\n223:   CLI entry (D10).\n224:   *Done when:* `bun run typecheck` is green; every name the item lists (`readPool / occupy /\n225:   prepare / stackEnv / release / evidence`) is exported; the guard (D5) runs on every\n226:   destructive path.\n227: - **S2. Setup run on the dev machine** — `bun tests/at/harness/db-pool.ts setup`; transcript\n228:   to `loop/items/AI4DEV-79/setup-pool.txt`.\n229:   *Done when:* `supabase status --workdir` for slot-1 and slot-2 reports loopback URLs on the\n230:   55321 and 56321 blocks respectively (this also settles F8's unverified `--workdir` claim);\n231:   a plain read of the personal stack's status still reports the 54321 block untouched. The\n232:   personal-stack check is a read; setup performs no write to it. The committed transcript is\n233:   scanned clean first (gate-1 [14]): no `eyJ` token, no anon or service-role key value, no\n234:   database password.\n235: - **S3. The isolation spike — AMENDED by ruling E5 (§8) after the incident; this text\n236:   replaces the original procedure, which stands in the history at commit 6429e7e.** Run once,\n237:   through the pool module's own `spike` CLI entry — never a hand-written script (E1).\n238:   Transcript to `loop/items/AI4DEV-79/spike-isolation-2.txt`; the first spike's transcript\n239:   stays committed as the incident record.\n240:   Hard constraint (founder, 2026-08-10): no command starts, stops, resets, connects to, or\n241:   writes to the personal stack. Docker READS (`docker ps`, `docker inspect`, `docker volume\n242:   ls` / `volume inspect`) are the only permitted interaction with it.\n243:   Hostile condition, mandatory: the spike parent process carries\n244:   `SUPABASE_PROJECT_ID=<the personal project id>` in its own environment — the exact override\n245:   that caused the breach — while the tracked `.env` stays in place unmodified. The transcript\n246:   prints that the variable was present in the parent.\n247:   Procedure: (a) BEFORE snapshot — docker-level identity record of every personal container\n248:   (container id, created timestamp, image, port bindings) and every personal volume (name,\n249:   CreatedAt); (b) preflight — status through the shared helper (D13) proves slot-1 and slot-2\n250:   answer on the 55321 and 56321 blocks; (c) canary row in slot-1's database; (d) canary row in\n251:   slot-2's database — the row the reset must DESTROY (gate-1 [7]); (e) the helper's\n252:   pre-destructive identity read on slot-2, visible in the transcript; (f) `db reset` aimed at\n253:   slot-2 through the helper, hostile env present; (g) slot-1 canary PRESENT and slot-2 canary\n254:   GONE; (h) AFTER snapshot — equal to (a) on every identity field: same container ids, same\n255:   created timestamps, same port bindings, same volume set, same volume CreatedAt values.\n256:   Run-state fields are excluded from the comparison: one personal container restart-loops on\n257:   its own (`vector`, observed on all three stacks, 2026-08-10), and run state is not the\n258:   breach signature — recreation is. Canary connections go only to slot ports; the spike opens\n259:   no connection to any 54321-block port.\n260:   *Done when:* the transcript shows the hostile variable present in the parent; the identity\n261:   pre-read naming slot-2's target before the reset; the slot-2 canary destroyed by the reset;\n262:   the slot-1 canary surviving it; and the before and after docker snapshots equal on every\n263:   identity field. The committed transcript is scanned clean per gate-1 [14]. The slot-2 reset\n264:   here also settles F4 for slots: the config references `./seed.sql`, the mirror reproduces\n265:   its absence (E3), and a reset that succeeds settles the claim on a slot identity this time.\n266: - **S4. `tests/at/harness/db-pool.selftest.ts`** — no Docker, temp pool roots via\n267:   `AT_DB_POOL_ROOT`, temp lock dirs via `AT_LOCK_DIR` (gate-1 [13]), runnable on CI (F7).\n268:   Named tests, each its own `it()`:\n269:   (1) two concurrent occupies on one slot → exactly one wins; (2) dead-pid claim → loud\n270:   takeover, and `tookOverFrom` recorded in the new claim; (3) the pool module's occupy/release\n271:   pair releases in a `finally` when its caller throws — unit level, the honest scope of a\n272:   Dockerless test (gate-1 [11]); (4) the evidence line carries slot + migration state; (5) the\n273:   personal-block guard refuses a 54321-block config and the repo project id; (6) no\n274:   reservation and no override → refusal that names `Reserve-DbSlot`; (7) the identity overlay\n275:   changes exactly the identity fields and nothing else (byte-compare the rest), including the\n276:   generalized port rule (an enabled `smtp_port` maps, an out-of-band port refuses — gate-1\n277:   [5]); (8) a LIVE holder is never taken over — dead-pid-only policy refuses and names the\n278:   holder (gate-1 [3]); (9) the branch parser fails closed — zero ids, one id, several ids\n279:   (gate-1 [12]).\n280:   *Done when:* `bun run at:selftest` is green including this file, on a machine with no\n281:   running stack.\n282: - **S5. The runner hook** (D8, D9) in `tests/at/harness/runner.ts`.\n283:   *Done when:* typecheck green; the loop-tier oracle holds (gate-1 [10]): the full output of\n284:   `bun run at:verify req-001 --tier loop --expect` captured on main and on the branch head,\n285:   volatile tokens normalized (durations, temp paths, dates), diff EMPTY, both normalized\n286:   transcripts and the diff committed and scanned clean per gate-1 [14]; the integration path\n287:   resolves the stack only through db-pool; the drill path refuses per D8; the pool claim is\n288:   stored in the same `lock` variable `cleanupRun` releases, so release stays in the existing\n289:   `finally` chain (gate-1 [11]).\n290: - **S6. Reservation helpers and process lines** — `loop/work/db-slots.ps1` (D10);\n291:   `.claude/skills/work/SKILL.md`: the claim step gains the admission-control line (reserve\n292:   before claiming; full pool → the item is REJECTED at start, stated; database-free items skip\n293:   the reservation, stated at start), the spawn-facts line gains the reserved slot as an item\n294:   fact, the sweep step gains `Release-DbSlot`.\n295:   *Done when:* the helpers dot-source and run under Windows PowerShell 5.1; `Reserve-DbSlot`\n296:   on a full pool returns a rejection, not a wait; `Release-DbSlot` refuses a reservation\n297:   naming a different item, and refuses while a live occupancy claim exists on the slot\n298:   (gate-1 [2]); the three SKILL.md touch points read correctly in context.\n299: - **S7. The record** — commit setup and spike transcripts and this plan's amendments as they\n300:   happen; every phase boundary pushes.\n301:   *Done when:* the item directory carries `setup-pool.txt` and `spike-isolation.txt` committed,\n302:   and `git status --porcelain` is empty at every sitting close.\n303: - **S8. End-to-end proof of the changed path** (gate-1 [9]) — goal phase, on the dev machine:\n304:   one real integration-tier verify through the pool, `bun run at:verify req-001 --tier\n305:   integration --expect` with `AT_DB_SLOT` set (the D6 override; this run is the evidence\n306:   gathering the override exists for). Transcript to\n307:   `loop/items/AI4DEV-79/integration-run.txt`.\n308:   *Done when:* the run is green, the evidence line names the slot, and the committed\n309:   transcript is scanned clean per gate-1 [14].\n310: \n311: ## 5. Expected verification state\n312: \n313: No acceptance-test ids exist on this item (foundation work). The goal state for the fix-and-goal\n314: sitting, all on the item branch:\n315: \n316: | check | expected |\n317: |---|---|\n318: | `bun run typecheck` | green |\n319: | `bun run at:selftest` | green, now including the nine named db-pool tests (S4) |\n320: | `bun run at:check req-001` | green, unchanged |\n321: | `bun run at:verify req-001 --tier loop --expect` | green, and the normalized main-vs-branch output diff is EMPTY (S5, gate-1 [10]) |\n322: | S8: one integration-tier verify via the pool, `AT_DB_SLOT` set | AMENDED by ruling X2 (§9): req-001 declares the loop tier only, so `--expect` refuses at integration; the criterion is the changed path end to end — occupancy, prepare, both identity instruments, the evidence line naming the slot, suite executed on the slot env, claim released — transcript committed (S8, gate-1 [9], X2) |\n323: | S3 spike done-criterion (amended, E5) | met once, transcript `spike-isolation-2.txt` committed — hostile env present, identity pre-read shown, slot-2 canary destroyed, slot-1 canary surviving, personal docker snapshots equal on every identity field |\n324: | every committed transcript | scanned clean: no `eyJ` token, no key value, no db password (gate-1 [14]) |\n325: | CI required check `verify` | green on the final head (it runs the first four rows; it cannot run the spike or the integration run — F7, no Docker) |\n326: \n327: What the green does NOT claim, stated now so no later phase inflates it: CI proves the pool's\n328: claim logic and guards on temp directories; only the committed setup, spike and\n329: integration-run transcripts prove real stacks, real ports, the real wall and the real runner\n330: path, and they prove it on the dev machine at the recorded commit, once.\n331: \n332: ## 6. Risks, residuals, and one reading made explicit\n333: \n334: - **The drill tier residual (F2, D8, amended by gate-1 [4]).** After this item, `--tier\n335:   drill` refuses as INFRASTRUCTURE instead of resetting the repo-config stack — the last\n336:   harness path that could reach the personal stack is closed in code. The coordinator still\n337:   gets a follow-up to file: decide the drill tier's stack, in words, as its own item; that\n338:   item replaces the refusal.\n339: - **The \"untouchable\" reading.** The ruled text makes the personal stack \"OUTSIDE the pool,\n340:   untouchable\", and the same ruled text's spike requires a canary row IN the personal stack.\n341:   These reconcile as: the POOL — its machinery, its resets, its tests — never targets the\n342:   personal stack's identity (D5 enforces this in code, S4 tests it); the one-time spike proves\n343:   that wall, and its personal-stack canary is the founder's own ruled proof, performed in a\n344:   scratch schema and removed, leaving zero residue (S3). The recurring selftests never open a\n345:   connection to any 54321-block port: they run on temp directories with no Docker at all.\n346:   SUPERSEDED 2026-08-10 by ruling E5 (§8): after the incident and the founder's answer, the\n347:   re-proof touches the personal stack not at all — its survival instrument is the docker-level\n348:   identity snapshot, and no canary ever enters the personal database. The paragraph above\n349:   stays as the record of the original reading.\n350: - **Config regeneration vs \"warm\" (D3).** A restart on functional-config drift is not a\n351:   reopening of the warm-slots ruling: warm remains the steady state; a restart happens only\n352:   when the item tree's own config differs from what the running slot was started with, which\n353:   is exactly when a warm stack would grade the wrong behavior.\n354: - **Two standing stacks cost real memory and containers.** Ruled (\"standing\"), so accepted;\n355:   the setup transcript records what actually started, so the cost is visible.\n356: - **Unverified runtime claims, routed to steps:** `--workdir` semantics (F8 → S2); reset with\n357:   a configured-but-absent seed file succeeds (F4 → S2/S3, the slot reset runs before any\n358:   suite depends on it); edge-runtime hot reload picks up functions copied after start (F5 →\n359:   S3 window or the first integration run — if false, prepare restarts the slot after the\n360:   copy, a contained change inside `prepare()`).\n361: - **Port collisions with unrelated software** on 55321/56321 blocks: `supabase start` fails\n362:   loudly at setup time; the setup transcript catches it on this machine now, not mid-item\n363:   later.\n364: - **The identity-read instrument is coupled to the CLI's output shape** (gate-2 [F4], §9). The\n365:   container-token scan and the personal-id substring check both read what the pinned CLI\n366:   (`supabase ^2.110.0`) prints in status output. A CLI upgrade that changes that shape must\n367:   re-prove the instrument. [T2]'s docker corroboration on the destructive path narrows the\n368:   exposure; it does not remove it.\n369: \n370: ## 7. Gate-1 rulings\n371: \n372: Written by the draft sitting (orchestrator, fable @ xhigh), 2026-08-10. Gate 1: sol\n373: (gpt-5.6-sol) via codex @ xhigh, sandbox read-only, 15 findings; distillate at\n374: `artifacts/gate1-sol-distillate.md`, count line matched. Every finding is ruled here, the claim\n375: quoted verbatim. The amended sections in §2–§6 carry the binding text; each ruling names them.\n376: Dispositions: 11 accepted, 4 accepted-fixed-differently, 0 rejected. No ruling removes work, so\n377: no removal verification conditions exist.\n378: \n379: **[1] ACCEPT** — claim: \"File equality cannot enforce D3's invariant that the on-disk config\n380: describes the successfully restarted stack.\"\n381: The crash window is real: a config write followed by a death before the restart leaves equal\n382: text and a stack still running the old behavior. Fix: prepare compares the regenerated config\n383: against a marker written only AFTER a successful start — `slot-N/.last-start.json` (config\n384: hash, timestamp, pid). Equal hash → keep the warm stack. Different or absent marker → write\n385: config, restart, and write the marker only when the restart succeeds. D3 amended.\n386: \n387: **[2] ACCEPT, FIXED DIFFERENTLY** — claim: \"D4 and D6 create separate reservation and occupancy\n388: files instead of the ruled single per-slot claim transitioning between reserved and occupied\n389: states.\"\n390: The races sol names are real and are fixed three ways: (i) `Release-DbSlot` deletes a\n391: reservation only when it names the item being swept — an ownership-checked release; (ii)\n392: `Release-DbSlot` refuses loudly while a live occupancy claim exists on that slot; (iii)\n393: `occupy` re-reads the reservation after the claim is acquired and releases-then-refuses if it\n394: no longer names the runner's item. The single-file shape itself is not adopted: the ruled\n395: text's own parenthetical describes occupancy as an \"atomic create-new claim\", and a create-new\n396: cannot target a file that already exists holding the reservation. The two ruled states are\n397: materialized as two files with one owner each; the three fixes close every gap sol names\n398: between them. D4 and D6 amended; S6's done-criterion extended.\n399: \n400: **[3] ACCEPT** — claim: \"Reusing `acquireStackLock` permits takeover of a live occupier after\n401: `LOCK_STALE_MINUTES`, not only takeover of a dead PID.\"\n402: Verified at runner.ts:285-288 this sitting. The ruled text says \"dead holder pid\" — the code\n403: must match it. Fix: `acquireStackLock` gains a takeover-policy parameter; the pool passes\n404: dead-pid-only, so a live holder is never taken over at any age — occupy refuses loudly and\n405: names the holder instead. Existing call sites keep today's default, so the runner selftests\n406: stand. D4 amended; S4 gains a named test.\n407: \n408: **[4] ACCEPT, FIXED DIFFERENTLY** — claim: \"D8 deliberately leaves the drill tier resetting the\n409: repo-configured personal stack despite the settled requirement that this stack remain\n410: untouchable.\"\n411: Verified this sitting: nothing in the tree invokes `--tier drill` — no script, no CI step, no\n412: process file; only type definitions and oracle-capability tests name it. Routing drill through\n413: the pool would widen the ruled hook, which is pinned to the integration tier — that is not this\n414: plan's call. So the drill tier REFUSES as INFRASTRUCTURE: \"the drill tier's stack is not yet\n415: decided\" — it no longer reaches any stack, personal or slot. Nothing breaks, and the reset path\n416: sol names is closed in code, not by a filed intention. The follow-up item that decides drill's\n417: stack replaces the refusal. D8 amended; the §6 residual is rewritten; the coordinator still\n418: gets the follow-up to file.\n419: \n420: **[5] ACCEPT** — claim: \"The identity overlay omits valid host-port fields such as\n421: `local_smtp.smtp_port` and `local_smtp.pop3_port` shown in `supabase/config.toml:110-111`.\"\n422: Verified: both keys exist, commented today, and an item may enable them. The overlay\n423: generalizes: every ACTIVE key named `port` or ending `_port`, in every section, with a value in\n424: 54000–54999, maps to value + N*1000; `edge_runtime.inspector_port` keeps its special case\n425: (8083 → 8083 + N*10); any other port value refuses loudly as unmappable. D5's personal-band\n426: scan covers every port-valued key, not a fixed seven. D2, D3 and D5 amended; S4 test 7 covers\n427: the generalization.\n428: \n429: **[6] ACCEPT, FIXED DIFFERENTLY** — claim: \"D7's fixed copy set is not the dependency closure\n430: of the item's regenerated config.\"\n431: The hazard is real; the proposed fixture-probe is not adopted — F6 already measured the\n432: missed-env behavior, and a probe proves one config once, not the rule. Fix instead, fail\n433: closed: after regeneration, prepare scans the config for active relative-path values (seed\n434: `sql_paths`, `schema_paths`, email template files, TLS and signing key paths) and refuses\n435: loudly on any path it cannot copy from the item tree — no stack starts half-provisioned. Env\n436: policy recorded as a decision: slot start and restart inherit the invoking process env; a\n437: missed `env()` resolves to a literal string and does not stop the stack (F6, measured). D7\n438: amended.\n439: \n440: **[7] ACCEPT** — claim: \"The isolation spike has no canary in slot 2 that must disappear.\"\n441: Correct, and the miss matters: without a vanishing canary, a no-op reset satisfies the whole\n442: done-criterion. The spike adds a canary row in slot-2 BEFORE the reset; the done-criterion\n443: requires it GONE after the reset, beside the two surviving canaries. S3 amended.\n444: \n445: **[8] ACCEPT** — claim: \"Personal scratch-schema cleanup is only the last procedural step, not\n446: guaranteed by a `finally`-style cleanup.\"\n447: The spike runs inside try/finally; the scratch-schema drop is the finally. A failed drop is\n448: reported loudly with the exact manual cleanup command. S3 amended.\n449: \n450: **[9] ACCEPT** — claim: \"No done-criterion executes the new integration runner path end to\n451: end.\"\n452: Correct — under the plan as written, the changed branch never runs before merge. New step S8\n453: (goal phase): one real integration-tier verify on the dev machine through the pool —\n454: `bun run at:verify req-001 --tier integration --expect` with `AT_DB_SLOT` set — green, the\n455: evidence line naming the slot, redacted transcript committed. §5 gains the row.\n456: \n457: **[10] ACCEPT** — claim: \"A green `--tier loop --expect` run does not prove byte-identical\n458: output against main.\"\n459: The oracle is now defined: capture the loop-tier verify's full output on main and on the branch\n460: head, normalize volatile tokens (durations, temp paths, dates), and diff. An empty diff is the\n461: done-criterion; both normalized transcripts and the diff are committed. S5 amended.\n462: \n463: **[11] ACCEPT, FIXED DIFFERENTLY** — claim: \"The Dockerless \\\"suite throws\\\" selftest has no\n464: specified seam capable of exercising the runner's occupied integration path.\"\n465: Correct that no seam exists without refactoring `main`, and a refactor made only for a test is\n466: not adopted. The test's claim narrows to what a Dockerless test can prove: the pool module's\n467: own occupy/release pair releases in a `finally` when its caller throws — unit level. The\n468: runner-level guarantee is delivered structurally: the pool path stores its claim in the SAME\n469: `lock` variable `cleanupRun` already releases (D9), S5's done-criterion pins that, and the\n470: draft-code gate reads it. S4 test 3 reworded.\n471: \n472: **[12] ACCEPT** — claim: \"D6 does not define a fail-closed branch parser for detached HEAD, no\n473: Git executable, or branches containing multiple item IDs.\"\n474: The parser becomes a pure function over the branch string: exactly one item id, or it refuses.\n475: Zero ids, several ids, detached HEAD, or a failed git call → refusal as INFRASTRUCTURE naming\n476: the condition. Never the first match. D6 amended; S4 gains parser tests (zero / one / many).\n477: \n478: **[13] ACCEPT** — claim: \"`AT_DB_POOL_ROOT` does not isolate the occupancy claim used by the\n479: proposed selftests.\"\n480: Verified: `lockDir()` (runner.ts:255-260) has no override. Fix: `lockDir` honors `AT_LOCK_DIR`\n481: (the same pattern as `AT_REPO_ROOT`); the default is unchanged; the db-pool selftests set it.\n482: D9 amended.\n483: \n484: **[14] ACCEPT** — claim: \"The committed setup transcript has no redaction or 'contains no\n485: credentials' done-criterion.\"\n486: Every committed transcript — S2 setup, S3 spike, S8 integration run, S5 baselines — passes a\n487: scan before commit: no `eyJ` token, no anon or service-role key value, no database password.\n488: The clean scan is part of each done-criterion. S2, S3, S5 and S8 amended.\n489: \n490: **[15] ACCEPT** — claim: \"F7 incorrectly states that the required check runs on\n491: `ubuntu-latest`.\"\n492: Verified at ci.yml:44: `runs-on: ${{ vars.CI_RUNNER_LABEL || 'ubuntu-latest' }}`. F7 is\n493: corrected: the check runs on ubuntu-latest, or on a self-hosted runner when that repository\n494: variable is set. The constraint this plan takes from F7 stands unchanged — every test this item\n495: adds must pass with no live stack and no Docker, on either runner.\n496: \n497: ## 8. INCIDENT — the spike destroyed the personal database (draft sitting, 2026-08-10)\n498: \n499: The isolation spike (S3), run by the draft executor, destroyed the founder's personal local\n500: database instead of proving the wall. This section records the measured facts, the rulings on\n501: them, and what is blocked until the founder decides. The orchestrator verified every fact below\n502: with its own instruments after the executor's report.\n503: \n504: ### The facts, measured\n505: \n506: - `supabase db reset` aimed at slot-2 — `--workdir <slot-2>` AND process working directory in\n507:   the slot — removed and recreated the container `supabase_db_poancmeitlmxejofwzuu`: the\n508:   founder's personal database, bound to slot-2's port 56322. The bind then failed (slot-2's\n509:   real container holds that port), so the personal db container now sits in state `Created`,\n510:   not running.\n511: - The personal db VOLUME was recreated at 2026-08-09T22:04:38Z, the moment of the reset; the\n512:   sibling personal volumes keep their 2026-08-08 timestamps. The old volume — the founder's\n513:   local data — is deleted. The schema is reproducible from `supabase/migrations`; hand-made\n514:   rows are not, unless a backup exists outside Docker that only the founder knows about.\n515: - Root cause: the tracked `.env` at the repo root, line 1, `SUPABASE_PROJECT_ID=\n516:   \"poancmeitlmxejofwzuu\"`. The Supabase CLI treats that variable as a `project_id` OVERRIDE,\n517:   so the slot's config supplied the ports while the environment supplied the personal\n518:   identity. The override reaches the CLI by two routes: the process environment, and env-file\n519:   loading in the invoking runtime. The hand-written spike script closed neither route; the\n520:   pool module's own `childEnv()` allowlist closed both, which is why S2's setup built correct\n521:   slot containers.\n522: - **F8 is settled AGAINST the plan: `--workdir` is NOT the wall.** D1's \"outside every\n523:   worktree\" layout stands, but nothing about `--workdir` isolates identity.\n524: - Collateral: F4's \"reset with a configured-but-absent seed succeeds\" verification is TAINTED\n525:   — the reset that appeared to succeed was acting on the personal identity. It returns to\n526:   unverified, settled by the re-run spike.\n527: - The slot-2 canary SURVIVED, proving the reset never touched slot-2 — gate-1 [7]'s vanishing\n528:   canary did exactly its job: without it, this spike could have read as a pass.\n529: \n530: ### Rulings\n531: \n532: - **E1. The wall is positive identity, never absence of an override.** Every slot CLI\n533:   invocation goes through ONE shared invocation helper in db-pool.ts — no role ever hand-rolls\n534:   a slot CLI command again, the spike script's exact mistake. The helper: (a) sets\n535:   `SUPABASE_PROJECT_ID=<slot project id>` explicitly in the child env; (b) strips every other\n536:   `SUPABASE_*` variable; (c) invokes `bun --no-env-file` per F8's house rule; (d) sets the\n537:   child working directory to the slot. Before any DESTRUCTIVE act, a read through the same\n538:   helper must prove the resolved target: the status the CLI reports carries the slot's project\n539:   id and ports — mismatch refuses loudly. D5's guard now bites at the layer that was actually\n540:   breached.\n541: - **E2. S3 and S5 are BLOCKED until the founder decides.** The spike must re-run to prove the\n542:   amended wall, and S5 is built only on a proven wall (the plan's own build-order rule). No\n543:   role starts, stops, resets or repairs the personal stack — recovery is the founder's alone.\n544: - **E3. Executor reading RATIFIED, amending D7 and gate-1 [6]:** the closure rule is \"mirror\n545:   `supabase/` entire; refuse on active references outside `supabase/`\". The literal per-path\n546:   refusal would false-refuse today's tree — `sql_paths = [\"./seed.sql\"]` is active and\n547:   `seed.sql` does not exist (F4); the mirror faithfully reproduces the absence.\n548: - **E4. Executor reading RATIFIED, amending D2's general port rule:** the overlay applies to\n549:   the local stack's LISTENER ports only (api, db, shadow, pooler, studio, local_smtp web/smtp/\n550:   pop3, analytics, edge_runtime inspector). A client-connection port to an external service —\n551:   e.g. a real `[auth.email.smtp] port = 587` — is data, passes through unchanged. D5's\n552:   personal-band refusal stays broad over all port keys: fail-closed and loud is correct there.\n553: \n554: ### Draft status at the interruption\n555: \n556: S1 done (typecheck green). S2 done, criteria met, transcript committed. S3 run and FAILED —\n557: done-criterion not met, wall disproven, transcript with postscript committed. S4, S5, S6 not\n558: started. The draft is incomplete; no draft-code gate can read it yet.\n559: \n560: ### Founder decisions and the re-proof ruling (resumed draft sitting, 2026-08-10)\n561: \n562: The founder answered, relayed verbatim by the conductor (2026-08-10, morning, founder local\n563: time): \"Personal can stay stopped. And continue 79.\" Mapped to the three questions above:\n564: \n565: 1. **Recovery:** no agent recovers, starts, stops, or repairs the personal stack. It stays as\n566:    the founder leaves it.\n567: 2. **Spike sequencing:** not ruled explicitly. The first answer fixes the CONSTRAINT — the\n568:    re-proof must not require starting the personal stack, and no agent may touch it. The shape\n569:    inside that constraint is the orchestrator's ruling: E5 below.\n570: 3. **Proceed:** yes — the item continues.\n571: \n572: Observed at sitting open (2026-08-10 09:30 founder local), recorded and not acted on: the\n573: personal db container was recreated at 2026-08-09T22:30:25Z — 26 minutes after the breach —\n574: and has run healthy on its correct port 54322 since; the recreated data volume dates\n575: 2026-08-09T22:25:17Z. Recovery happened outside the item, before the founder's morning answer.\n576: Recovery is the founder's alone (E2), so this is consistent, and no item agent touched the\n577: stack. Ruling E5 makes the re-proof independent of the personal stack's run state, so this\n578: observation changes no step; it corrects the \"container dead in state Created\" line above,\n579: which described 2026-08-09 22:04–22:30 only.\n580: \n581: - **E5. The re-proof shape: a zero-touch pass under the hostile condition.** The amended S3\n582:   (§4) proves the wall with zero interaction with the personal stack beyond docker-level\n583:   reads. Three instruments replace the original personal-stack canary:\n584:   (1) the vanishing slot-2 canary and the surviving slot-1 canary — reset scope, exactly as\n585:   gate-1 [7] required; (2) the shared helper's pre-destructive identity read (E1/D13) —\n586:   positive identity, visible in the transcript; (3) a docker-level before/after snapshot of\n587:   every personal container and volume, compared on identity fields: container id, created\n588:   timestamp, port bindings, volume name set, volume CreatedAt. The breach signature was\n589:   RECREATION — the incident recreated the db container and its volume — so identity-field\n590:   equality is the direct negative of the breach.\n591:   **Why no disposable stand-in plays the personal stack's part:** docker object names derive\n592:   from the project id, so a stand-in carrying the personal project id IS the personal stack's\n593:   container set — creating or removing it would itself touch what must stay untouched — and a\n594:   stand-in under any other id is just another bystander, a role slot-1 already fills. A\n595:   stand-in adds cost and no proof.\n596:   **Why the proof does not depend on the personal stack's run state:** the CLI selects the\n597:   docker objects it acts on by resolved project identity, not by what is running — the\n598:   incident demonstrated this by destroying the personal identity's containers while running\n599:   slot-2 went untouched. A leak under the amended helper surfaces as one of: the identity\n600:   pre-read reporting the wrong target (refusal before the reset), the reset acting on the\n601:   personal identity (snapshot mismatch), or the reset failing to act on slot-2 (the canary\n602:   survives and the done-criterion fails). No leak shape produces a green transcript.\n603:   **The hostile condition is mandatory:** the spike parent deliberately carries the `.env`\n604:   override that caused the breach, so a pass proves the helper closes the route WHILE the\n605:   route is loaded — not that the route happened to be empty on the day.\n606: - **E2 amended: UNBLOCKED (founder, 2026-08-10).** S3 as amended runs now; S5 follows only\n607:   after S3's done-criterion is met, per the plan's build-order rule. The no-touch rule on the\n608:   personal stack is unchanged and permanent.\n609: \n610: ### Draft-pass ratifications (resumed draft sitting, 2026-08-10)\n611: \n612: The draft executor (opus) completed E1/D13 with E3 and E4 folded, the amended S3, S4, S5 and\n613: S6 in one invocation. The spike ran twice inside it: the first run stopped on a leftover\n614: canary table in slot 1's database — a client problem, no identity resolved, the personal\n615: snapshot already IDENTICAL on that attempt — and the postscript of\n616: `loop/items/AI4DEV-79/spike-isolation-2.txt` records both runs. The wall is re-proven under\n617: the hostile condition; the orchestrator read the transcript and confirmed the personal\n618: stack's docker identity with its own instrument. Four executor judgment calls, ruled:\n619: \n620: - **E6. The identity pre-read instrument — RATIFIED.** E1's \"any project identity the CLI\n621:   reports matches the slot id\" clause is implemented as a strict scan over every `supabase_*`\n622:   container-name token in the CLI's own status output: every token must end in the slot's\n623:   project id, the personal project id must appear nowhere, and `localStackProblems` must pass\n624:   against the slot's config. The executor's observation is correct and recorded: ports alone\n625:   would NOT have caught the incident — the hybrid invocation reported slot-2's ports\n626:   correctly; the container names carry the RESOLVED project. The pre-read stays a\n627:   corroborating instrument; the wall itself is the positive `SUPABASE_PROJECT_ID` plus the\n628:   working directory (E1).\n629: - **E7. The scan's fail-closed bias — ACCEPTED.** A `supabase_*` token that does not end in\n630:   the slot's project id refuses loudly, so an unknown token false-refuses rather than passes.\n631:   A false refusal is loud and costs a look; a false pass destroyed a database. Consistent\n632:   with D2's unmappable-port refusal and D6's fail-closed parser.\n633: - **E8. Two selftests beyond the nine named — KEPT.** The positive reservation-lookup case\n634:   and the guard-accepts-a-generated-slot-config case exist so the refusal tests cannot pass\n635:   vacuously. Gate 1's central theme was oracles too weak to prove their claim; these apply\n636:   that lesson.\n637: - **E9. Watcher-file churn — RATIFIED as housekeeping.** The conductor's remote-tip watcher\n638:   (`artifacts/watch-tip.sh`, a tracked file) rewrites its own base pointer after every push,\n639:   so the tree goes dirty moments after each close. The executor committed the arrived change\n640:   unaltered, labelled as not-item work; this sitting did the same. Recorded here so the\n641:   change-scope check downstream reads these commits as declared, not as scope drift.\n642: \n643: ## 9. Gate-2 rulings (fix sitting, orchestrator on fable, 2026-08-10)\n644: \n645: Gate 2 was a panel of two, each blind to the other. Reader one: terra via codex @ max,\n646: read-only sandbox — 13 findings, distillate `artifacts/gate2-terra-distillate.md`, count line\n647: matched. Reader two: flash via opencode, agent `reviewer-flash`, variant max — 9 findings plus\n648: four notes outside the findings, distillate `artifacts/gate2-flash-distillate.md`, count line\n649: matched. Every finding is ruled here with the claim quoted verbatim. Neither seat outvotes the\n650: other; convergences are named because a panel converging on one defect is its strongest signal.\n651: \n652: Dispositions: terra — 8 accepted, 4 accepted-fixed-differently, 1 rejected. flash — 9 accepted,\n653: 0 rejected; all four notes ruled below. No ruling removes work, so no removal verification\n654: conditions exist. One ruling ([T2]) carries a verify-first component for the executor.\n655: \n656: ### Terra's findings\n657: \n658: **[T1] ACCEPT — the critical.** Claim: \"An empty or partially written lock file is treated as a\n659: dead holder under `dead-pid-only`, so a second occupier can delete a live process's just-created\n660: claim.\"\n661: Verified at runner.ts:440: `readHolder` returns `{}` for an unreadable or half-written file, and\n662: `holderIsLive({})` is false under every policy — so an unidentifiable holder is takeover-eligible\n663: even under the policy whose ruled meaning is \"a DEAD HOLDER PID and nothing else\". The window is\n664: the microseconds between `openSync(file, 'wx')` and the `writeSync`, and it is real. Fix, scoped\n665: to `dead-pid-only`: an unidentifiable holder is NEVER takeover-eligible. After one bounded\n666: re-read (a short pause, then read again — enough to skate over the write window), a holder that\n667: still has no parseable pid refuses loudly, naming the file and the manual deletion path. The\n668: same guard applies to the re-read INSIDE the takeover gate, so the gate cannot remove an\n669: unidentifiable file either. `stale-or-dead` keeps today's behaviour: that path predates this\n670: item and is another item's business if anyone wants it changed.\n671: \n672: **[T2] ACCEPT, with a verify-first component.** Claim: \"`proveSlotTarget` accepts a valid status\n673: result containing zero `supabase_*` container tokens, so its project-identity check can pass\n674: vacuously.\"\n675: True by code: `foreignContainerNames` on empty input returns nothing, `carriesPersonal` is\n\n(Output capped at 50 KB. Showing lines 1-675. Use offset=676 to continue.)\n</content>",
loop/items/AI4DEV-79/artifacts/audit-luna-output.stderr.log:11450:C:\Users\nirdr\Downloads\ai4good\.claude\worktrees\agent-a064eb77d7bb37dd7\loop\items\AI4DEV-79\artifacts\audit-flash-identity.raw.json:158: "text": "# Plan — AI4DEV-79 (parallel local DB slot pool)\n\nWritten by the plan sitting (orchestrator, fable @ xhigh), 2026-08-10. Chain, derived from the\nbranch: AI4DEV-79 (parallel DB slot pool) → AI4DEV-3 (AT harness), root label `attr:bringup`.\nFoundation work: no requirement above it, no acceptance-test ids of its own.\n\n## 1. The ruled design — quoted from the board item, because the board is not in the tree\n\nThe Linear description carries the founder-ruled design (ruled 2026-08-09). This gate critiques\nthe mechanism below it; it does not reopen these decisions. The ruled points, quoted:\n\n> * **A pool of 2 standing Supabase stacks** (\"slots\"), each with its own project id and port\n>   block. Concurrency = pool size. The default stack on ports 54321+ is the founder's personal\n>   stack and stays OUTSIDE the pool, untouchable.\n> * **Warm slots**: the stacks stay running. (Flipping to start-on-acquire later is a one-line\n>   change.)\n> * **One claim file per slot, two states, three owners**: the coordinator RESERVES a slot when\n>   an item starts (`/work` time — admission control); the harness runner OCCUPIES it for each\n>   verify window (atomic create-new claim, pid stamped, release in a `finally`); the\n>   coordinator RELEASES the reservation at the item sweep.\n> * **Full pool = the next database-needing item is REJECTED at start.** No flagged limbo.\n>   Items that need no database (docs, contracts) skip the reservation, stated at start.\n> * **State is never inherited.** First act of every occupancy: copy the item tree's own\n>   `supabase/migrations` + seed into the slot directory and run `supabase db reset --workdir\n>   <slot>`. The previous holder is never trusted, including a crashed one.\n> * **Stale claims are broken LOUDLY** — dead holder pid, takeover recorded in the claim, never\n>   silent.\n> * **The evidence names the slot**: the verify transcript carries one line — slot, reset\n>   performed, migration state. A green that cannot name its reset ran against unknown state.\n> * **Identity/data split**: each slot directory permanently owns its `config.toml` (project id\n>   + ports = identity); the item supplies migrations by copy (data). The repo's committed\n>   `supabase/config.toml` is never edited, so no item diff is ever polluted.\n\nWhat gets built, per the item: (1) a one-time setup script; (2) `tests/at/harness/db-pool.ts`\n(~150–250 lines) exporting `readPool / occupy / prepare / stackEnv / release / evidence`;\n(3) one hook in `tests/at/harness/runner.ts` gated behind the integration tier, loop-tier\nbehavior byte-identical; (4) reservation helpers (PowerShell, work library) plus reservation\nlines in the /work skill, the conductor's spawn facts, and release in the coordinator sweep.\n\nThe tests, per the item: `db-pool.selftest.ts` with named tests (two concurrent occupies on one\nslot → exactly one wins; dead-pid claim → loud takeover, recorded; release fires from `finally`\neven when the suite throws; the evidence line carries slot + migration state), and:\n\n> **The isolation spike comes FIRST and is a done-criterion**: canary rows in slot-1 AND in the\n> personal stack must survive a `db reset` aimed at slot-2. Never assume the wall — prove it\n> before building on it.\n\n> Suites change ZERO lines — they already reach the stack only through the env the runner\n> injects.\n\n## 2. Facts about the tree this plan stands on (verified this sitting; pointers, not paste)\n\n- F1. The runner's integration-tier sequence is `tests/at/harness/runner.ts` main(), the\n  `tier !== 'loop'` block (~lines 1044–1112): read `supabase/config.toml` at REPO_ROOT →\n  machine-wide stack lock → `supabase status` → prove-local checks → readiness → `db reset\n  --local` → readiness → prove migrations replayed → inject `AT_SUPABASE_URL`,\n  `AT_SUPABASE_DB_URL`, `AT_SUPABASE_ANON_KEY`, `AT_SUPABASE_SERVICE_ROLE_KEY` into the vitest\n  child. Suites see only that env (runner.ts ~1108–1111). The item's \"suites change ZERO lines\"\n  claim is true against the tree.\n- F2. The tiers are `loop | integration | drill` (runner.ts:47). The `tier !== 'loop'` guard\n  means the DRILL tier also reaches the repo-config stack today.\n- F3. `acquireStackLock` (runner.ts ~343–442) is already an atomic create-new claim file, pid\n  and start-time stamped, with a race-proven stale-takeover gate (runner.selftest.ts) and a\n  loud console line on takeover. Its release runs inside the runner's `finally` chain\n  (`cleanupRun`, runner.ts ~938–946, called from main's `finally`). The lock file lives at\n  `<LOCALAPPDATA|XDG_CACHE_HOME|tmpdir>/ai4good-build/at-locks/at-verify-<projectId>-<apiPort>.lock`.\n- F4. The personal stack's identity (`supabase/config.toml`): `project_id\n  \"poancmeitlmxejofwzuu\"`; ports 54321 (api), 54322 (db), 54320 (shadow), 54323 (studio),\n  54324 (local_smtp), 54327 (analytics), 54329 (pooler, disabled), and `edge_runtime`\n  `inspector_port` 8083. Seed config `[db.seed] sql_paths = [\"./seed.sql\"]` — and\n  `supabase/seed.sql` does NOT exist in the tree today.\n- F5. `supabase/functions/` carries two live edge functions plus `_shared` modules, and\n  `config.toml` pins `verify_jwt = true` for both. A slot that lacks the functions directory\n  serves 404 where the personal stack serves the function.\n- F6. `config.toml` uses `env()` substitution for OAuth credentials; a missed env resolves to a\n  literal string and does not stop the stack (documented in config.toml ~line 410, measured on\n  the sign-in item).\n- F7. CI (`.github/workflows/ci.yml`, required check `verify`) runs on `ubuntu-latest`, or on\n  a self-hosted runner when the repository variable `CI_RUNNER_LABEL` is set (ci.yml:44;\n  corrected by gate-1 [15]), with NO Docker step either way: typecheck, `at:selftest` (vitest\n  over `tests/at/harness/`), `at:check` per suite, `at:verify --tier loop --expect` per\n  declaration. Every test this item adds to `harness/` must be green with no live stack, no\n  Docker, and no Windows-only path assumption.\n- F8. The Supabase CLI is pinned in devDependencies (`supabase ^2.110.0`) and always invoked as\n  `bun --no-env-file <INSTALL_ROOT>/node_modules/supabase/dist/supabase.js` (runner.ts:69,\n  460–463). The CLI takes a global `--workdir` flag naming a directory that contains a\n  `supabase/` project folder — used by nothing in this repo yet, so it is UNVERIFIED here;\n  step S2's done-criterion proves it before anything depends on it.\n- F9. The work library pattern for locks is `Acquire-WorkLock` / `Release-WorkLock`\n  (`loop/work/work-lib.ps1` ~196–223). The /work skill's claim step and spawn-facts step are\n  SKILL.md steps 4–6 (~lines 91–101); the coordinator sweep step is SKILL.md ~line 170.\n- F10. The verify surface for this item (foundation work, no AT ids): `bun run typecheck`,\n  `bun run at:selftest`, `bun run at:check req-001`, `bun run at:verify req-001 --tier loop\n  --expect` — the same four things CI runs, and all four are green on main today.\n\n## 3. Decisions — the mechanism under the ruled design\n\n- **D1. Pool root.** `<LOCALAPPDATA ?? XDG_CACHE_HOME ?? tmpdir>/ai4good-build/db-slots/`,\n  the same base-resolution rule as the runner's `lockDir()` (F3), so the pool lives beside the\n  locks it cooperates with, outside every worktree. Layout: `slot-1/supabase/…`,\n  `slot-2/supabase/…`, `reservations/slot-N.json`. Env override `AT_DB_POOL_ROOT` exists for\n  the selftests only, exactly as `AT_REPO_ROOT` exists for the runner's own tests.\n- **D2. Slot identity.** Slot N owns `project_id \"ai4good-slot-N\"` and the port overlay\n  `54xxx + N*1000`: slot-1 = 55320/55321/55322/55323/55324/55327/55329, inspector 8093;\n  slot-2 = 56320/56321/56322/56323/56324/56327/56329, inspector 8103. Distinct project ids\n  give distinct Docker container and volume names; distinct port blocks give the wall the\n  spike proves. No slot value may fall in 54320–54329 or be 8083 (D5 enforces).\n  The overlay rule is GENERAL (gate-1 [5]): every active key named `port` or ending `_port`,\n  in every section, with a value in 54000–54999, maps to value + N*1000.\n  `edge_runtime.inspector_port` maps 8083 → 8083 + N*10. Any other active port value refuses\n  loudly as unmappable — a human decides, not a guess.\n- **D3. Slot config regeneration — identity is permanent, everything else is data.** The ruled\n  identity/data split names project id + ports as the slot's identity. Auth flags, seed paths\n  and `verify_jwt` pins also live in `config.toml`, and items change them (F4's\n  `enable_confirmations` was flipped by an item). A slot config frozen at setup time would\n  drift from the item tree's config, and a verify on a slot would grade different auth\n  behavior than a verify on the stack the config describes. So `prepare()` regenerates the\n  slot's `config.toml` on every occupancy: take the item tree's `supabase/config.toml`\n  verbatim, overlay ONLY the identity fields (project_id, the seven port keys, inspector_port)\n  with the slot's permanent values, using the same section-aware line scan `readLocalConfig`\n  already uses, and the general port rule in D2. The keep-warm decision compares against a\n  MARKER, not the file (gate-1 [1]): `slot-N/.last-start.json` holds the hash of the config the\n  stack last STARTED with, written only after a successful start. Regenerated hash equals the\n  marker → nothing happens, the warm stack keeps running (the ruled default). Different or\n  absent marker → prepare writes the config, restarts that slot's stack (`stop` + `start\n  --workdir`) before the reset — the auth container reads config at start, not at reset — and\n  writes the marker only when the restart succeeds. A crash between the write and the restart\n  therefore forces a restart on the next occupancy instead of hiding behind equal text.\n- **D4. Occupancy = the existing stack lock, extended.** `occupy()` acquires\n  `acquireStackLock(slotConfig, requirement)` (F3) — it is already the atomic create-new,\n  pid-stamped, stale-takeover-with-loud-line claim the ruled text describes, and it is already\n  released in the runner's `finally` chain. Two extensions. First: when a stale claim is\n  broken, the new claim file records `tookOverFrom` (the dead holder's pid and start time), so\n  the takeover is recorded IN the claim, not only on the console. Second (gate-1 [3]):\n  `acquireStackLock` gains a takeover-policy parameter; the pool passes dead-pid-only, so a\n  LIVE holder is never taken over at any age — occupy refuses loudly and names the holder.\n  Existing call sites keep today's default policy unchanged.\n- **D5. The personal block is refused in code, not by convention.** db-pool refuses to occupy,\n  prepare, reset or emit env for any slot whose config carries the repo config's `project_id`,\n  any port in 54320–54329, or inspector port 8083. The scan covers EVERY active port-valued\n  key in the config, not a fixed list (gate-1 [5]). The check runs before anything\n  destructive, every time, and has its own named selftest. This is the \"untouchable\" ruling as\n  an executable guard.\n- **D6. Reservation lookup is derived, never declared.** The coordinator's `Reserve-DbSlot`\n  writes `reservations/slot-N.json` (exclusive create; content: item id, branch, timestamp,\n  holder). At verify time the runner derives its item id from the current branch (the house\n  attribution rule) and occupies the slot whose reservation names that item. A run with no\n  reservation for its item refuses as INFRASTRUCTURE, naming `Reserve-DbSlot` — no silent\n  fallback onto any free slot, because admission control is the coordinator's, not the\n  runner's. `AT_DB_SLOT=N` is the explicit override for runs outside an item (the founder, the\n  evidence gate, the spike); the occupancy claim still applies.\n  Three hardenings (gate-1 [2], [12]): the branch parser is a pure function over the branch\n  string and FAILS CLOSED — exactly one item id, or it refuses naming the condition (zero ids,\n  several ids, detached HEAD, git call failed); `occupy` re-reads the reservation AFTER the\n  claim is acquired and releases-then-refuses if it no longer names the runner's item;\n  `Release-DbSlot` deletes a reservation only when it names the item being swept, and refuses\n  loudly while a live occupancy claim exists on that slot.\n- **D7. Data copy at occupancy.** Before the reset, prepare deletes and re-copies into the\n  slot: `supabase/migrations/*.sql` (timestamped files only, the runner's own pattern),\n  `supabase/seed.sql` when the item tree has one — and removes the slot's copy when it does\n  not (F4) — and `supabase/functions/**` (F5: integration suites can call edge functions\n  through the injected URL). The previous holder's files are never trusted; delete-then-copy,\n  not merge.\n  Fail closed on the closure (gate-1 [6]): after regeneration, prepare scans the config for\n  active relative-path values (seed `sql_paths`, `schema_paths`, email template files, TLS and\n  signing key paths) and refuses loudly on any path it cannot copy from the item tree — no\n  stack starts half-provisioned. Env policy, decided: slot start and restart inherit the\n  invoking process env; a missed `env()` resolves to a literal string and does not stop the\n  stack (F6, measured). [The inherit-env sentence is SUPERSEDED by ruling E1's strip — see §9,\n  flash note 1: every `env()` substitution resolves to the literal string on a slot, always.]\n- **D8. The runner hook is integration-tier only.** In main's stack block: when\n  `tier === 'integration'`, resolve the stack through the pool — occupy → prepare (copy,\n  config, reset via `--workdir`, readiness, prove migrations) → the same prove-local checks\n  against the SLOT's config → inject the slot's env — and print the evidence line. The loop\n  tier stays byte-identical (it never enters the block). The DRILL tier REFUSES as\n  INFRASTRUCTURE (gate-1 [4]): \"the drill tier's stack is not yet decided\" — it no longer\n  reaches any stack, personal or slot. Nothing in the tree invokes `--tier drill` today\n  (verified: no script, no CI step, no process file), so nothing breaks, and the last harness\n  path that could reset the personal stack is closed in code. The follow-up item that decides\n  drill's stack replaces the refusal.\n- **D9. Existing helpers are parameterized, not duplicated.** `readLocalConfig`,\n  `readStackStatus`, `resetLocalDatabase` and `proveMigrationsReplayed` currently hard-code\n  REPO_ROOT (F1). Each gains a workdir/config parameter defaulting to today's value —\n  loop-tier call-site behavior is unchanged — and the pool path passes the slot.\n  No second copy of the status parser, the prove-local checks or the reset process handling.\n  `lockDir()` honors an `AT_LOCK_DIR` env override (gate-1 [13]), the same pattern as\n  `AT_REPO_ROOT`; the default is unchanged; the db-pool selftests set it so their claims never\n  touch the machine-wide lock directory.\n- **D10. One place builds configs.** All TOML reading and generation lives in db-pool.ts\n  (TypeScript). The one-time setup is a CLI entry in the same file (`bun\n  tests/at/harness/db-pool.ts setup`, the `import.meta.main` pattern runner.ts uses): create\n  both slot directories, generate both configs from the current tree + identity overlay, start\n  both stacks via the pinned CLI, print both status reports. The PowerShell helpers\n  (`loop/work/db-slots.ps1`: `Reserve-DbSlot`, `Release-DbSlot`, `Get-DbSlotPool`) do file\n  operations only — reserve, release, list — mirroring the `Acquire-WorkLock` house pattern\n  (F9). No TOML logic in PowerShell, so the overlay cannot fork.\n- **D11. The evidence line.** One line on the verify transcript, printed by the runner on the\n  pool path: `at:verify — db slot <N> (<project_id>, api <port>) — reset OK — migrations: <E>\n  expected, <A> applied`. Slot, reset performed, migration state — the ruled three.\n- **D12. No slicing.** The diff is one mechanism — pool module, its selftests, one runner\n  hook, small PowerShell helpers, and process-file lines. The parts reference each other; a\n  per-slice code gate would review the claim logic apart from the tests that pin it. One\n  draft-code gate reads the whole diff.\n- **D13. One shared invocation helper (ruling E1, §8; added by the resumed draft sitting,\n  2026-08-10).** Every slot CLI invocation goes through one helper in db-pool.ts. The helper:\n  sets `SUPABASE_PROJECT_ID=<slot project id>` positively in the child env; strips every other\n  `SUPABASE_*` variable; invokes `bun --no-env-file` per the house rule; sets the child working\n  directory to the slot. The destructive path (reset, stop) performs an identity read through\n  the same helper first — the status the CLI reports must carry the slot's ports, and any\n  project identity it reports must be the slot's — and refuses loudly on mismatch. The read is\n  structurally ON the destructive path, never a separate call a caller can skip. This applies\n  to every slot-aimed use of the parameterized runner helpers (D9) too.\n\n## 4. Steps, each with its done-criterion\n\nBuild order note: the ruled text says the spike comes FIRST — before anything is built ON the\nwall. The spike needs the slots to exist, so S1–S2 create them, S3 proves the wall, and only\nthen S5 builds the runner hook on top of it.\n\n- **S1. `tests/at/harness/db-pool.ts`** — pool root resolution (D1), config read/overlay/\n  generation (D2, D3), `readPool`, `occupy` (D4), `prepare` (D3, D7), `stackEnv`, `release`,\n  `evidence` (D11), the personal-block guard (D5), reservation lookup (D6), and the `setup`\n  CLI entry (D10).\n  *Done when:* `bun run typecheck` is green; every name the item lists (`readPool / occupy /\n  prepare / stackEnv / release / evidence`) is exported; the guard (D5) runs on every\n  destructive path.\n- **S2. Setup run on the dev machine** — `bun tests/at/harness/db-pool.ts setup`; transcript\n  to `loop/items/AI4DEV-79/setup-pool.txt`.\n  *Done when:* `supabase status --workdir` for slot-1 and slot-2 reports loopback URLs on the\n  55321 and 56321 blocks respectively (this also settles F8's unverified `--workdir` claim);\n  a plain read of the personal stack's status still reports the 54321 block untouched. The\n  personal-stack check is a read; setup performs no write to it. The committed transcript is\n  scanned clean first (gate-1 [14]): no `eyJ` token, no anon or service-role key value, no\n  database password.\n- **S3. The isolation spike — AMENDED by ruling E5 (§8) after the incident; this text\n  replaces the original procedure, which stands in the history at commit 6429e7e.** Run once,\n  through the pool module's own `spike` CLI entry — never a hand-written script (E1).\n  Transcript to `loop/items/AI4DEV-79/spike-isolation-2.txt`; the first spike's transcript\n  stays committed as the incident record.\n  Hard constraint (founder, 2026-08-10): no command starts, stops, resets, connects to, or\n  writes to the personal stack. Docker READS (`docker ps`, `docker inspect`, `docker volume\n  ls` / `volume inspect`) are the only permitted interaction with it.\n  Hostile condition, mandatory: the spike parent process carries\n  `SUPABASE_PROJECT_ID=<the personal project id>` in its own environment — the exact override\n  that caused the breach — while the tracked `.env` stays in place unmodified. The transcript\n  prints that the variable was present in the parent.\n  Procedure: (a) BEFORE snapshot — docker-level identity record of every personal container\n  (container id, created timestamp, image, port bindings) and every personal volume (name,\n  CreatedAt); (b) preflight — status through the shared helper (D13) proves slot-1 and slot-2\n  answer on the 55321 and 56321 blocks; (c) canary row in slot-1's database; (d) canary row in\n  slot-2's database — the row the reset must DESTROY (gate-1 [7]); (e) the helper's\n  pre-destructive identity read on slot-2, visible in the transcript; (f) `db reset` aimed at\n  slot-2 through the helper, hostile env present; (g) slot-1 canary PRESENT and slot-2 canary\n  GONE; (h) AFTER snapshot — equal to (a) on every identity field: same container ids, same\n  created timestamps, same port bindings, same volume set, same volume CreatedAt values.\n  Run-state fields are excluded from the comparison: one personal container restart-loops on\n  its own (`vector`, observed on all three stacks, 2026-08-10), and run state is not the\n  breach signature — recreation is. Canary connections go only to slot ports; the spike opens\n  no connection to any 54321-block port.\n  *Done when:* the transcript shows the hostile variable present in the parent; the identity\n  pre-read naming slot-2's target before the reset; the slot-2 canary destroyed by the reset;\n  the slot-1 canary surviving it; and the before and after docker snapshots equal on every\n  identity field. The committed transcript is scanned clean per gate-1 [14]. The slot-2 reset\n  here also settles F4 for slots: the config references `./seed.sql`, the mirror reproduces\n  its absence (E3), and a reset that succeeds settles the claim on a slot identity this time.\n- **S4. `tests/at/harness/db-pool.selftest.ts`** — no Docker, temp pool roots via\n  `AT_DB_POOL_ROOT`, temp lock dirs via `AT_LOCK_DIR` (gate-1 [13]), runnable on CI (F7).\n  Named tests, each its own `it()`:\n  (1) two concurrent occupies on one slot → exactly one wins; (2) dead-pid claim → loud\n  takeover, and `tookOverFrom` recorded in the new claim; (3) the pool module's occupy/release\n  pair releases in a `finally` when its caller throws — unit level, the honest scope of a\n  Dockerless test (gate-1 [11]); (4) the evidence line carries slot + migration state; (5) the\n  personal-block guard refuses a 54321-block config and the repo project id; (6) no\n  reservation and no override → refusal that names `Reserve-DbSlot`; (7) the identity overlay\n  changes exactly the identity fields and nothing else (byte-compare the rest), including the\n  generalized port rule (an enabled `smtp_port` maps, an out-of-band port refuses — gate-1\n  [5]); (8) a LIVE holder is never taken over — dead-pid-only policy refuses and names the\n  holder (gate-1 [3]); (9) the branch parser fails closed — zero ids, one id, several ids\n  (gate-1 [12]).\n  *Done when:* `bun run at:selftest` is green including this file, on a machine with no\n  running stack.\n- **S5. The runner hook** (D8, D9) in `tests/at/harness/runner.ts`.\n  *Done when:* typecheck green; the loop-tier oracle holds (gate-1 [10]): the full output of\n  `bun run at:verify req-001 --tier loop --expect` captured on main and on the branch head,\n  volatile tokens normalized (durations, temp paths, dates), diff EMPTY, both normalized\n  transcripts and the diff committed and scanned clean per gate-1 [14]; the integration path\n  resolves the stack only through db-pool; the drill path refuses per D8; the pool claim is\n  stored in the same `lock` variable `cleanupRun` releases, so release stays in the existing\n  `finally` chain (gate-1 [11]).\n- **S6. Reservation helpers and process lines** — `loop/work/db-slots.ps1` (D10);\n  `.claude/skills/work/SKILL.md`: the claim step gains the admission-control line (reserve\n  before claiming; full pool → the item is REJECTED at start, stated; database-free items skip\n  the reservation, stated at start), the spawn-facts line gains the reserved slot as an item\n  fact, the sweep step gains `Release-DbSlot`.\n  *Done when:* the helpers dot-source and run under Windows PowerShell 5.1; `Reserve-DbSlot`\n  on a full pool returns a rejection, not a wait; `Release-DbSlot` refuses a reservation\n  naming a different item, and refuses while a live occupancy claim exists on the slot\n  (gate-1 [2]); the three SKILL.md touch points read correctly in context.\n- **S7. The record** — commit setup and spike transcripts and this plan's amendments as they\n  happen; every phase boundary pushes.\n  *Done when:* the item directory carries `setup-pool.txt` and `spike-isolation.txt` committed,\n  and `git status --porcelain` is empty at every sitting close.\n- **S8. End-to-end proof of the changed path** (gate-1 [9]) — goal phase, on the dev machine:\n  one real integration-tier verify through the pool, `bun run at:verify req-001 --tier\n  integration --expect` with `AT_DB_SLOT` set (the D6 override; this run is the evidence\n  gathering the override exists for). Transcript to\n  `loop/items/AI4DEV-79/integration-run.txt`.\n  *Done when:* the run is green, the evidence line names the slot, and the committed\n  transcript is scanned clean per gate-1 [14].\n\n## 5. Expected verification state\n\nNo acceptance-test ids exist on this item (foundation work). The goal state for the fix-and-goal\nsitting, all on the item branch:\n\n| check | expected |\n|---|---|\n| `bun run typecheck` | green |\n| `bun run at:selftest` | green, now including the nine named db-pool tests (S4) |\n| `bun run at:check req-001` | green, unchanged |\n| `bun run at:verify req-001 --tier loop --expect` | green, and the normalized main-vs-branch output diff is EMPTY (S5, gate-1 [10]) |\n| S8: one integration-tier verify via the pool, `AT_DB_SLOT` set | AMENDED by ruling X2 (§9): req-001 declares the loop tier only, so `--expect` refuses at integration; the criterion is the changed path end to end — occupancy, prepare, both identity instruments, the evidence line naming the slot, suite executed on the slot env, claim released — transcript committed (S8, gate-1 [9], X2) |\n| S3 spike done-criterion (amended, E5) | met once, transcript `spike-isolation-2.txt` committed — hostile env present, identity pre-read shown, slot-2 canary destroyed, slot-1 canary surviving, personal docker snapshots equal on every identity field |\n| every committed transcript | scanned clean: no `eyJ` token, no key value, no db password (gate-1 [14]) |\n| CI required check `verify` | green on the final head (it runs the first four rows; it cannot run the spike or the integration run — F7, no Docker) |\n\nWhat the green does NOT claim, stated now so no later phase inflates it: CI proves the pool's\nclaim logic and guards on temp directories; only the committed setup, spike and\nintegration-run transcripts prove real stacks, real ports, the real wall and the real runner\npath, and they prove it on the dev machine at the recorded commit, once.\n\n## 6. Risks, residuals, and one reading made explicit\n\n- **The drill tier residual (F2, D8, amended by gate-1 [4]).** After this item, `--tier\n  drill` refuses as INFRASTRUCTURE instead of resetting the repo-config stack — the last\n  harness path that could reach the personal stack is closed in code. The coordinator still\n  gets a follow-up to file: decide the drill tier's stack, in words, as its own item; that\n  item replaces the refusal.\n- **The \"untouchable\" reading.** The ruled text makes the personal stack \"OUTSIDE the pool,\n  untouchable\", and the same ruled text's spike requires a canary row IN the personal stack.\n  These reconcile as: the POOL — its machinery, its resets, its tests — never targets the\n  personal stack's identity (D5 enforces this in code, S4 tests it); the one-time spike proves\n  that wall, and its personal-stack canary is the founder's own ruled proof, performed in a\n  scratch schema and removed, leaving zero residue (S3). The recurring selftests never open a\n  connection to any 54321-block port: they run on temp directories with no Docker at all.\n  SUPERSEDED 2026-08-10 by ruling E5 (§8): after the incident and the founder's answer, the\n  re-proof touches the personal stack not at all — its survival instrument is the docker-level\n  identity snapshot, and no canary ever enters the personal database. The paragraph above\n  stays as the record of the original reading.\n- **Config regeneration vs \"warm\" (D3).** A restart on functional-config drift is not a\n  reopening of the warm-slots ruling: warm remains the steady state; a restart happens only\n  when the item tree's own config differs from what the running slot was started with, which\n  is exactly when a warm stack would grade the wrong behavior.\n- **Two standing stacks cost real memory and containers.** Ruled (\"standing\"), so accepted;\n  the setup transcript records what actually started, so the cost is visible.\n- **Unverified runtime claims, routed to steps:** `--workdir` semantics (F8 → S2); reset with\n  a configured-but-absent seed file succeeds (F4 → S2/S3, the slot reset runs before any\n  suite depends on it); edge-runtime hot reload picks up functions copied after start (F5 →\n  S3 window or the first integration run — if false, prepare restarts the slot after the\n  copy, a contained change inside `prepare()`).\n- **Port collisions with unrelated software** on 55321/56321 blocks: `supabase start` fails\n  loudly at setup time; the setup transcript catches it on this machine now, not mid-item\n  later.\n- **The identity-read instrument is coupled to the CLI's output shape** (gate-2 [F4], §9). The\n  container-token scan and the personal-id substring check both read what the pinned CLI\n  (`supabase ^2.110.0`) prints in status output. A CLI upgrade that changes that shape must\n  re-prove the instrument. [T2]'s docker corroboration on the destructive path narrows the\n  exposure; it does not remove it.\n\n## 7. Gate-1 rulings\n\nWritten by the draft sitting (orchestrator, fable @ xhigh), 2026-08-10. Gate 1: sol\n(gpt-5.6-sol) via codex @ xhigh, sandbox read-only, 15 findings; distillate at\n`artifacts/gate1-sol-distillate.md`, count line matched. Every finding is ruled here, the claim\nquoted verbatim. The amended sections in §2–§6 carry the binding text; each ruling names them.\nDispositions: 11 accepted, 4 accepted-fixed-differently, 0 rejected. No ruling removes work, so\nno removal verification conditions exist.\n\n**[1] ACCEPT** — claim: \"File equality cannot enforce D3's invariant that the on-disk config\ndescribes the successfully restarted stack.\"\nThe crash window is real: a config write followed by a death before the restart leaves equal\ntext and a stack still running the old behavior. Fix: prepare compares the regenerated config\nagainst a marker written only AFTER a successful start — `slot-N/.last-start.json` (config\nhash, timestamp, pid). Equal hash → keep the warm stack. Different or absent marker → write\nconfig, restart, and write the marker only when the restart succeeds. D3 amended.\n\n**[2] ACCEPT, FIXED DIFFERENTLY** — claim: \"D4 and D6 create separate reservation and occupancy\nfiles instead of the ruled single per-slot claim transitioning between reserved and occupied\nstates.\"\nThe races sol names are real and are fixed three ways: (i) `Release-DbSlot` deletes a\nreservation only when it names the item being swept — an ownership-checked release; (ii)\n`Release-DbSlot` refuses loudly while a live occupancy claim exists on that slot; (iii)\n`occupy` re-reads the reservation after the claim is acquired and releases-then-refuses if it\nno longer names the runner's item. The single-file shape itself is not adopted: the ruled\ntext's own parenthetical describes occupancy as an \"atomic create-new claim\", and a create-new\ncannot target a file that already exists holding the reservation. The two ruled states are\nmaterialized as two files with one owner each; the three fixes close every gap sol names\nbetween them. D4 and D6 amended; S6's done-criterion extended.\n\n**[3] ACCEPT** — claim: \"Reusing `acquireStackLock` permits takeover of a live occupier after\n`LOCK_STALE_MINUTES`, not only takeover of a dead PID.\"\nVerified at runner.ts:285-288 this sitting. The ruled text says \"dead holder pid\" — the code\nmust match it. Fix: `acquireStackLock` gains a takeover-policy parameter; the pool passes\ndead-pid-only, so a live holder is never taken over at any age — occupy refuses loudly and\nnames the holder instead. Existing call sites keep today's default, so the runner selftests\nstand. D4 amended; S4 gains a named test.\n\n**[4] ACCEPT, FIXED DIFFERENTLY** — claim: \"D8 deliberately leaves the drill tier resetting the\nrepo-configured personal stack despite the settled requirement that this stack remain\nuntouchable.\"\nVerified this sitting: nothing in the tree invokes `--tier drill` — no script, no CI step, no\nprocess file; only type definitions and oracle-capability tests name it. Routing drill through\nthe pool would widen the ruled hook, which is pinned to the integration tier — that is not this\nplan's call. So the drill tier REFUSES as INFRASTRUCTURE: \"the drill tier's stack is not yet\ndecided\" — it no longer reaches any stack, personal or slot. Nothing breaks, and the reset path\nsol names is closed in code, not by a filed intention. The follow-up item that decides drill's\nstack replaces the refusal. D8 amended; the §6 residual is rewritten; the coordinator still\ngets the follow-up to file.\n\n**[5] ACCEPT** — claim: \"The identity overlay omits valid host-port fields such as\n`local_smtp.smtp_port` and `local_smtp.pop3_port` shown in `supabase/config.toml:110-111`.\"\nVerified: both keys exist, commented today, and an item may enable them. The overlay\ngeneralizes: every ACTIVE key named `port` or ending `_port`, in every section, with a value in\n54000–54999, maps to value + N*1000; `edge_runtime.inspector_port` keeps its special case\n(8083 → 8083 + N*10); any other port value refuses loudly as unmappable. D5's personal-band\nscan covers every port-valued key, not a fixed seven. D2, D3 and D5 amended; S4 test 7 covers\nthe generalization.\n\n**[6] ACCEPT, FIXED DIFFERENTLY** — claim: \"D7's fixed copy set is not the dependency closure\nof the item's regenerated config.\"\nThe hazard is real; the proposed fixture-probe is not adopted — F6 already measured the\nmissed-env behavior, and a probe proves one config once, not the rule. Fix instead, fail\nclosed: after regeneration, prepare scans the config for active relative-path values (seed\n`sql_paths`, `schema_paths`, email template files, TLS and signing key paths) and refuses\nloudly on any path it cannot copy from the item tree — no stack starts half-provisioned. Env\npolicy recorded as a decision: slot start and restart inherit the invoking process env; a\nmissed `env()` resolves to a literal string and does not stop the stack (F6, measured). D7\namended.\n\n**[7] ACCEPT** — claim: \"The isolation spike has no canary in slot 2 that must disappear.\"\nCorrect, and the miss matters: without a vanishing canary, a no-op reset satisfies the whole\ndone-criterion. The spike adds a canary row in slot-2 BEFORE the reset; the done-criterion\nrequires it GONE after the reset, beside the two surviving canaries. S3 amended.\n\n**[8] ACCEPT** — claim: \"Personal scratch-schema cleanup is only the last procedural step, not\nguaranteed by a `finally`-style cleanup.\"\nThe spike runs inside try/finally; the scratch-schema drop is the finally. A failed drop is\nreported loudly with the exact manual cleanup command. S3 amended.\n\n**[9] ACCEPT** — claim: \"No done-criterion executes the new integration runner path end to\nend.\"\nCorrect — under the plan as written, the changed branch never runs before merge. New step S8\n(goal phase): one real integration-tier verify on the dev machine through the pool —\n`bun run at:verify req-001 --tier integration --expect` with `AT_DB_SLOT` set — green, the\nevidence line naming the slot, redacted transcript committed. §5 gains the row.\n\n**[10] ACCEPT** — claim: \"A green `--tier loop --expect` run does not prove byte-identical\noutput against main.\"\nThe oracle is now defined: capture the loop-tier verify's full output on main and on the branch\nhead, normalize volatile tokens (durations, temp paths, dates), and diff. An empty diff is the\ndone-criterion; both normalized transcripts and the diff are committed. S5 amended.\n\n**[11] ACCEPT, FIXED DIFFERENTLY** — claim: \"The Dockerless \\\"suite throws\\\" selftest has no\nspecified seam capable of exercising the runner's occupied integration path.\"\nCorrect that no seam exists without refactoring `main`, and a refactor made only for a test is\nnot adopted. The test's claim narrows to what a Dockerless test can prove: the pool module's\nown occupy/release pair releases in a `finally` when its caller throws — unit level. The\nrunner-level guarantee is delivered structurally: the pool path stores its claim in the SAME\n`lock` variable `cleanupRun` already releases (D9), S5's done-criterion pins that, and the\ndraft-code gate reads it. S4 test 3 reworded.\n\n**[12] ACCEPT** — claim: \"D6 does not define a fail-closed branch parser for detached HEAD, no\nGit executable, or branches containing multiple item IDs.\"\nThe parser becomes a pure function over the branch string: exactly one item id, or it refuses.\nZero ids, several ids, detached HEAD, or a failed git call → refusal as INFRASTRUCTURE naming\nthe condition. Never the first match. D6 amended; S4 gains parser tests (zero / one / many).\n\n**[13] ACCEPT** — claim: \"`AT_DB_POOL_ROOT` does not isolate the occupancy claim used by the\nproposed selftests.\"\nVerified: `lockDir()` (runner.ts:255-260) has no override. Fix: `lockDir` honors `AT_LOCK_DIR`\n(the same pattern as `AT_REPO_ROOT`); the default is unchanged; the db-pool selftests set it.\nD9 amended.\n\n**[14] ACCEPT** — claim: \"The committed setup transcript has no redaction or 'contains no\ncredentials' done-criterion.\"\nEvery committed transcript — S2 setup, S3 spike, S8 integration run, S5 baselines — passes a\nscan before commit: no `eyJ` token, no anon or service-role key value, no database password.\nThe clean scan is part of each done-criterion. S2, S3, S5 and S8 amended.\n\n**[15] ACCEPT** — claim: \"F7 incorrectly states that the required check runs on\n`ubuntu-latest`.\"\nVerified at ci.yml:44: `runs-on: ${{ vars.CI_RUNNER_LABEL || 'ubuntu-latest' }}`. F7 is\ncorrected: the check runs on ubuntu-latest, or on a self-hosted runner when that repository\nvariable is set. The constraint this plan takes from F7 stands unchanged — every test this item\nadds must pass with no live stack and no Docker, on either runner.\n\n## 8. INCIDENT — the spike destroyed the personal database (draft sitting, 2026-08-10)\n\nThe isolation spike (S3), run by the draft executor, destroyed the founder's personal local\ndatabase instead of proving the wall. This section records the measured facts, the rulings on\nthem, and what is blocked until the founder decides. The orchestrator verified every fact below\nwith its own instruments after the executor's report.\n\n### The facts, measured\n\n- `supabase db reset` aimed at slot-2 — `--workdir <slot-2>` AND process working directory in\n  the slot — removed and recreated the container `supabase_db_poancmeitlmxejofwzuu`: the\n  founder's personal database, bound to slot-2's port 56322. The bind then failed (slot-2's\n  real container holds that port), so the personal db container now sits in state `Created`,\n  not running.\n- The personal db VOLUME was recreated at 2026-08-09T22:04:38Z, the moment of the reset; the\n  sibling personal volumes keep their 2026-08-08 timestamps. The old volume — the founder's\n  local data — is deleted. The schema is reproducible from `supabase/migrations`; hand-made\n  rows are not, unless a backup exists outside Docker that only the founder knows about.\n- Root cause: the tracked `.env` at the repo root, line 1, `SUPABASE_PROJECT_ID=\n  \"poancmeitlmxejofwzuu\"`. The Supabase CLI treats that variable as a `project_id` OVERRIDE,\n  so the slot's config supplied the ports while the environment supplied the personal\n  identity. The override reaches the CLI by two routes: the process environment, and env-file\n  loading in the invoking runtime. The hand-written spike script closed neither route; the\n  pool module's own `childEnv()` allowlist closed both, which is why S2's setup built correct\n  slot containers.\n- **F8 is settled AGAINST the plan: `--workdir` is NOT the wall.** D1's \"outside every\n  worktree\" layout stands, but nothing about `--workdir` isolates identity.\n- Collateral: F4's \"reset with a configured-but-absent seed succeeds\" verification is TAINTED\n  — the reset that appeared to succeed was acting on the personal identity. It returns to\n  unverified, settled by the re-run spike.\n- The slot-2 canary SURVIVED, proving the reset never touched slot-2 — gate-1 [7]'s vanishing\n  canary did exactly its job: without it, this spike could have read as a pass.\n\n### Rulings\n\n- **E1. The wall is positive identity, never absence of an override.** Every slot CLI\n  invocation goes through ONE shared invocation helper in db-pool.ts — no role ever hand-rolls\n  a slot CLI command again, the spike script's exact mistake. The helper: (a) sets\n  `SUPABASE_PROJECT_ID=<slot project id>` explicitly in the child env; (b) strips every other\n  `SUPABASE_*` variable; (c) invokes `bun --no-env-file` per F8's house rule; (d) sets the\n  child working directory to the slot. Before any DESTRUCTIVE act, a read through the same\n  helper must prove the resolved target: the status the CLI reports carries the slot's project\n  id and ports — mismatch refuses loudly. D5's guard now bites at the layer that was actually\n  breached.\n- **E2. S3 and S5 are BLOCKED until the founder decides.** The spike must re-run to prove the\n  amended wall, and S5 is built only on a proven wall (the plan's own build-order rule). No\n  role starts, stops, resets or repairs the personal stack — recovery is the founder's alone.\n- **E3. Executor reading RATIFIED, amending D7 and gate-1 [6]:** the closure rule is \"mirror\n  `supabase/` entire; refuse on active references outside `supabase/`\". The literal per-path\n  refusal would false-refuse today's tree — `sql_paths = [\"./seed.sql\"]` is active and\n  `seed.sql` does not exist (F4); the mirror faithfully reproduces the absence.\n- **E4. Executor reading RATIFIED, amending D2's general port rule:** the overlay applies to\n  the local stack's LISTENER ports only (api, db, shadow, pooler, studio, local_smtp web/smtp/\n  pop3, analytics, edge_runtime inspector). A client-connection port to an external service —\n  e.g. a real `[auth.email.smtp] port = 587` — is data, passes through unchanged. D5's\n  personal-band refusal stays broad over all port keys: fail-closed and loud is correct there.\n\n### Draft status at the interruption\n\nS1 done (typecheck green). S2 done, criteria met, transcript committed. S3 run and FAILED —\ndone-criterion not met, wall disproven, transcript with postscript committed. S4, S5, S6 not\nstarted. The draft is incomplete; no draft-code gate can read it yet.\n\n### Founder decisions and the re-proof ruling (resumed draft sitting, 2026-08-10)\n\nThe founder answered, relayed verbatim by the conductor (2026-08-10, morning, founder local\ntime): \"Personal can stay stopped. And continue 79.\" Mapped to the three questions above:\n\n1. **Recovery:** no agent recovers, starts, stops, or repairs the personal stack. It stays as\n   the founder leaves it.\n2. **Spike sequencing:** not ruled explicitly. The first answer fixes the CONSTRAINT — the\n   re-proof must not require starting the personal stack, and no agent may touch it. The shape\n   inside that constraint is the orchestrator's ruling: E5 below.\n3. **Proceed:** yes — the item continues.\n\nObserved at sitting open (2026-08-10 09:30 founder local), recorded and not acted on: the\npersonal db container was recreated at 2026-08-09T22:30:25Z — 26 minutes after the breach —\nand has run healthy on its correct port 54322 since; the recreated data volume dates\n2026-08-09T22:25:17Z. Recovery happened outside the item, before the founder's morning answer.\nRecovery is the founder's alone (E2), so this is consistent, and no item agent touched the\nstack. Ruling E5 makes the re-proof independent of the personal stack's run state, so this\nobservation changes no step; it corrects the \"container dead in state Created\" line above,\nwhich described 2026-08-09 22:04–22:30 only.\n\n- **E5. The re-proof shape: a zero-touch pass under the hostile condition.** The amended S3\n  (§4) proves the wall with zero interaction with the personal stack beyond docker-level\n  reads. Three instruments replace the original personal-stack canary:\n  (1) the vanishing slot-2 canary and the surviving slot-1 canary — reset scope, exactly as\n  gate-1 [7] required; (2) the shared helper's pre-destructive identity read (E1/D13) —\n  positive identity, visible in the transcript; (3) a docker-level before/after snapshot of\n  every personal container and volume, compared on identity fields: container id, created\n  timestamp, port bindings, volume name set, volume CreatedAt. The breach signature was\n  RECREATION — the incident recreated the db container and its volume — so identity-field\n  equality is the direct negative of the breach.\n  **Why no disposable stand-in plays the personal stack's part:** docker object names derive\n  from the project id, so a stand-in carrying the personal project id IS the personal stack's\n  container set — creating or removing it would itself touch what must stay untouched — and a\n  stand-in under any other id is just another bystander, a role slot-1 already fills. A\n  stand-in adds cost and no proof.\n  **Why the proof does not depend on the personal stack's run state:** the CLI selects the\n  docker objects it acts on by resolved project identity, not by what is running — the\n  incident demonstrated this by destroying the personal identity's containers while running\n  slot-2 went untouched. A leak under the amended helper surfaces as one of: the identity\n  pre-read reporting the wrong target (refusal before the reset), the reset acting on the\n  personal identity (snapshot mismatch), or the reset failing to act on slot-2 (the canary\n  survives and the done-criterion fails). No leak shape produces a green transcript.\n  **The hostile condition is mandatory:** the spike parent deliberately carries the `.env`\n  override that caused the breach, so a pass proves the helper closes the route WHILE the\n  route is loaded — not that the route happened to be empty on the day.\n- **E2 amended: UNBLOCKED (founder, 2026-08-10).** S3 as amended runs now; S5 follows only\n  after S3's done-criterion is met, per the plan's build-order rule. The no-touch rule on the\n  personal stack is unchanged and permanent.\n\n### Draft-pass ratifications (resumed draft sitting, 2026-08-10)\n\nThe draft executor (opus) completed E1/D13 with E3 and E4 folded, the amended S3, S4, S5 and\nS6 in one invocation. The spike ran twice inside it: the first run stopped on a leftover\ncanary table in slot 1's database — a client problem, no identity resolved, the personal\nsnapshot already IDENTICAL on that attempt — and the postscript of\n`loop/items/AI4DEV-79/spike-isolation-2.txt` records both runs. The wall is re-proven under\nthe hostile condition; the orchestrator read the transcript and confirmed the personal\nstack's docker identity with its own instrument. Four executor judgment calls, ruled:\n\n- **E6. The identity pre-read instrument — RATIFIED.** E1's \"any project identity the CLI\n  reports matches the slot id\" clause is implemented as a strict scan over every `supabase_*`\n  container-name token in the CLI's own status output: every token must end in the slot's\n  project id, the personal project id must appear nowhere, and `localStackProblems` must pass\n  against the slot's config. The executor's observation is correct and recorded: ports alone\n  would NOT have caught the incident — the hybrid invocation reported slot-2's ports\n  correctly; the container names carry the RESOLVED project. The pre-read stays a\n  corroborating instrument; the wall itself is the positive `SUPABASE_PROJECT_ID` plus the\n  working directory (E1).\n- **E7. The scan's fail-closed bias — ACCEPTED.** A `supabase_*` token that does not end in\n  the slot's project id refuses loudly, so an unknown token false-refuses rather than passes.\n  A false refusal is loud and costs a look; a false pass destroyed a database. Consistent\n  with D2's unmappable-port refusal and D6's fail-closed parser.\n- **E8. Two selftests beyond the nine named — KEPT.** The positive reservation-lookup case\n  and the guard-accepts-a-generated-slot-config case exist so the refusal tests cannot pass\n  vacuously. Gate 1's central theme was oracles too weak to prove their claim; these apply\n  that lesson.\n- **E9. Watcher-file churn — RATIFIED as housekeeping.** The conductor's remote-tip watcher\n  (`artifacts/watch-tip.sh`, a tracked file) rewrites its own base pointer after every push,\n  so the tree goes dirty moments after each close. The executor committed the arrived change\n  unaltered, labelled as not-item work; this sitting did the same. Recorded here so the\n  change-scope check downstream reads these commits as declared, not as scope drift.\n\n## 9. Gate-2 rulings (fix sitting, orchestrator on fable, 2026-08-10)\n\nGate 2 was a panel of two, each blind to the other. Reader one: terra via codex @ max,\nread-only sandbox — 13 findings, distillate `artifacts/gate2-terra-distillate.md`, count line\nmatched. Reader two: flash via opencode, agent `reviewer-flash`, variant max — 9 findings plus\nfour notes outside the findings, distillate `artifacts/gate2-flash-distillate.md`, count line\nmatched. Every finding is ruled here with the claim quoted verbatim. Neither seat outvotes the\nother; convergences are named because a panel converging on one defect is its strongest signal.\n\nDispositions: terra — 8 accepted, 4 accepted-fixed-differently, 1 rejected. flash — 9 accepted,\n0 rejected; all four notes ruled below. No ruling removes work, so no removal verification\nconditions exist. One ruling ([T2]) carries a verify-first component for the executor.\n\n### Terra's findings\n\n**[T1] ACCEPT — the critical.** Claim: \"An empty or partially written lock file is treated as a\ndead holder under `dead-pid-only`, so a second occupier can delete a live process's just-created\nclaim.\"\nVerified at runner.ts:440: `readHolder` returns `{}` for an unreadable or half-written file, and\n`holderIsLive({})` is false under every policy — so an unidentifiable holder is takeover-eligible\neven under the policy whose ruled meaning is \"a DEAD HOLDER PID and nothing else\". The window is\nthe microseconds between `openSync(file, 'wx')` and the `writeSync`, and it is real. Fix, scoped\nto `dead-pid-only`: an unidentifiable holder is NEVER takeover-eligible. After one bounded\nre-read (a short pause, then read again — enough to skate over the write window), a holder that\nstill has no parseable pid refuses loudly, naming the file and the manual deletion path. The\nsame guard applies to the re-read INSIDE the takeover gate, so the gate cannot remove an\nunidentifiable file either. `stale-or-dead` keeps today's behaviour: that path predates this\nitem and is another item's business if anyone wants it changed.\n\n**[T2] ACCEPT, with a verify-first component.** Claim: \"`proveSlotTarget` accepts a valid status\nresult containing zero `supabase_*` container tokens, so its project-identity check can pass\nvacuously.\"\nTrue by code: `foreignContainerNames` on empty input returns nothing, `carriesPersonal` is",
loop/items/AI4DEV-79/artifacts/audit-luna-output.stderr.log:11453:C:\Users\nirdr\Downloads\ai4good\.claude\worktrees\agent-a064eb77d7bb37dd7\loop\items\AI4DEV-79\artifacts\audit-flash-identity.raw.json:301: "output": "<path>C:\\Users\\nirdr\\Downloads\\ai4good\\.claude\\worktrees\\agent-a064eb77d7bb37dd7\\loop\\items\\AI4DEV-79\\plan.md</path>\n<type>file</type>\n<content>\n676: false, and the read proceeds on port checks alone — the exact hybrid shape the incident wore\n677: (right ports, wrong project) passes if the CLI prints no container names. Fail-closed (E7) says\n678: absence of identity evidence must refuse a destructive act. VERIFY FIRST (executor, read-only):\n679: run `status -o json` through the helper on a slot and measure whether `supabase_*` tokens appear\n680: in the combined output. Then: destructive acts require POSITIVE identity evidence — at least one\n681: `supabase_*` token ending in the slot's project id when the measurement shows tokens appear; and\n682: in every case a docker READ (`docker ps` filtered on the slot's own project id) must confirm the\n683: slot's own db container exists before a reset. Docker reads are the one permitted instrument on\n684: the untouchable stack and are unrestricted on slots. This also narrows [F4]'s residual: the CLI\n685: output shape is no longer the only instrument.\n686: \n687: **[T3] ACCEPT — amends ruling E3 (convergence with flash [F3]).** Claim: \"The full-tree mirror\n688: copies ignored `supabase/.temp` and `.branches` runtime state into slots.\"\n689: Both seats found this independently. `supabase/.temp` is the CLI's runtime state about the\n690: PERSONAL stack — and `.gitignore` itself says its `start-secrets/**` \"must NEVER be committed\" —\n691: so the mirror carries another stack's identity residue and secrets into every slot. E3's\n692: \"mirror entire, no filter\" is narrowed, not removed: the mirror carries the item tree's PROJECT\n693: SOURCE entire; the CLI's own runtime directories `.temp` and `.branches` are not project source\n694: and are excluded, as is `config.toml` (see [T11]). `pathClosureProblems` gains the matching\n695: refusal: a config path that resolves into `.temp` or `.branches` refuses loudly, so the closure\n696: guarantee stays honest about the exclusions.\n697: \n698: **[T4] ACCEPT (convergence with flash note 2).** Claim: \"The `spike` command resets slot 2\n699: without acquiring either slot's occupancy claim.\"\n700: D6's own ruled sentence — \"the occupancy claim still applies\" for override runs — is not\n701: implemented by the spike, which calls `resetSlotDatabase` and writes canaries with no claim\n702: held. Fix: the spike occupies both slots through the normal `occupy` path (dead-pid-only\n703: policy) before touching either, and releases both in a `finally`.\n704: \n705: **[T5] ACCEPT, FIXED DIFFERENTLY.** Claim: \"`AT_DB_SLOT` bypasses branch-derived reservation\n706: ownership and the post-claim reservation reread even when invoked from a normal item branch.\"\n707: The framing over-claims: slot STATE is disposable by design (\"state is never inherited\" — every\n708: occupancy resets from its own tree), so the wall against destruction is the occupancy claim,\n709: which the override keeps, not the reservation, which is admission control. The override itself\n710: is ruled (D6: the founder, the evidence gate, the spike). But a silent stomp on a slot another\n711: item holds is real and cheap to refuse: `occupy` via override now refuses loudly when the\n712: target slot carries a reservation naming a DIFFERENT item than this run can derive (from\n713: `options.item` or the branch); a run that can derive no item treats ANY reservation as foreign.\n714: The refusal names the reservation holder. An unreserved slot, or one reserved for this run's\n715: own item, proceeds as today.\n716: \n717: **[T6] ACCEPT.** Claim: \"Occupancy locks the previous slot config, while `prepare` can rewrite\n718: that config's listener ports from the current item tree.\"\n719: The claim file's name derives from `projectId + apiPort` read from the slot's CURRENT on-disk\n720: config, which `prepare` may lawfully rewrite — so two runs bracketing a port change would hold\n721: two different lock files for one slot. Fix: slot claims are keyed on the slot's PERMANENT\n722: identity — project id `ai4good-slot-N` plus a fixed sentinel port — used by `occupy` and by\n723: `readPool`'s occupancy read, and documented at the key. The PowerShell occupancy glob\n724: (`at-verify-ai4good-slot-N-*.lock`) already matches any suffix and is unchanged.\n725: \n726: **[T7] ACCEPT (convergence with flash [F1] — same line, both seats).** Claim:\n727: \"`resetSlotDatabase`, `stopSlotStack`, and `stackEnv` do not run the broad D5 personal-block\n728: guard themselves.\"\n729: D5's ruled sentence names \"occupy, prepare, reset or emit env\"; the code runs `refusePersonal`\n730: on the first two only, and the last two are exported entry points a caller can reach directly —\n731: the spike does. Fix: each of the three runs `refusePersonal` over the slot's on-disk config\n732: text before acting. `stackEnv` keeps its inline URL-port checks besides — they check the\n733: STATUS, which the config guard cannot see.\n734: \n735: **[T8] ACCEPT, FIXED DIFFERENTLY — one half of the claim is wrong and is recorded as such.**\n736: Claim: \"`edge_runtime.inspector_port` is remapped from any value, including non-numeric values,\n737: instead of refusing every value other than the ruled 8083.\"\n738: The non-numeric half is false: the `!literal` branch at db-pool.ts:242 refuses before the\n739: inspector case is reached. The numeric half is right: any numeric inspector value is remapped\n740: by +N*10, which is a guess for every value except the ruled 8083, and a guess is what D2\n741: forbids. Fix: the special case pins to exactly 8083; any other inspector value falls through to\n742: the generic listener rule — in-band maps, out-of-band refuses loudly.\n743: \n744: **[T9] REJECT.** Claim: \"`Release-DbSlot` checks occupancy and deletes the reservation without\n745: an atomic handoff.\"\n746: The TOCTOU window is real and changes nothing destructive. The serializer for destructive acts\n747: is the occupancy claim (dead-pid-only; a live holder is never displaced): a runner that slips\n748: into the window still holds its claim, so the worst outcome is the NEXT item's occupy refusing\n749: loudly until the window closes — a loud refusal, not a reset under a live run. An atomic\n750: two-file handoff in PowerShell 5.1 would buy no safety the claim does not already provide.\n751: Recorded as a residual beside the sweep helper's comment.\n752: \n753: **[T10] ACCEPT.** Claim: \"Two concurrent `Reserve-DbSlot` calls for the same item can reserve\n754: both slots.\"\n755: True by walk-through: both pass the already-held scan, one wins slot 1, the loser's catch\n756: records \"held\" and advances to slot 2, and one item holds the whole pool. Fix: in the\n757: create-failure catch, re-read the reservation; when it names the same item, return\n758: `alreadyHeld` for that slot instead of advancing.\n759: \n760: **[T11] ACCEPT, FIXED DIFFERENTLY.** Claim: \"An interruption between delete/copy and\n761: regenerated-config write can permanently strand a slot before the marker recovery logic runs.\"\n762: Ruling this surfaced something worse than stranding: between the mirror and the config write,\n763: the slot's `config.toml` IS the item tree's config — the personal identity — on disk in a slot\n764: directory. A crash in that window leaves a slot wearing the personal identity (occupy would\n765: refuse loudly, but the file should never exist at all). Fix: the mirror excludes `config.toml`\n766: (with [T3]'s exclusions, one mechanism) and the generated config is written immediately after —\n767: so no slot config ever carries the personal identity, even transiently, and a crash in the\n768: window leaves NO config rather than a wrong one. The missing-config refusal in `occupy` names\n769: both causes (never set up; a prepare that died mid-window) and the one-command repair. Not\n770: permanent, and loud at every step.\n771: \n772: **[T12] ACCEPT (convergence with flash [F2], second half).** Claim: \"The personal Docker\n773: snapshot can report `IDENTICAL` when both before/after queries return zero containers and\n774: volumes.\"\n775: A vacuous pass on the spike's central instrument — gate 1's whole theme. Fix: a new spike\n776: criterion requires the BEFORE snapshot non-empty (at least one personal container AND one\n777: personal volume recorded); an empty snapshot fails the run.\n778: \n779: **[T13] ACCEPT, FIXED DIFFERENTLY.** Claim: \"The path-closure check validates lexical paths but\n780: does not resolve symlinks under `supabase/`.\"\n781: Real, and resolving symlinks lexically is the wrong instrument. Fix, fail-closed and total: the\n782: mirror refuses loudly on ANY symlink found under the item tree's `supabase/` before copying.\n783: The tree carries none today (the executor confirms the scan passes), so nothing breaks, and a\n784: symlink smuggled in later refuses instead of copying as a link that points wherever it likes.\n785: \n786: ### Flash's findings\n787: \n788: **[F1] ACCEPT — same defect as [T7], one fix.** Claim: \"The D5 personal-block guard\n789: (`refusePersonal`) runs on occupy/prepare/stackEnv but NOT on the exported destructive entry\n790: points `resetSlotDatabase` and `stopSlotStack` — those run only the identity read.\"\n791: Both seats converged on this line independently. Ruled at [T7]; flash's tampered-config probe\n792: becomes unnecessary once the guard is structural.\n793: \n794: **[F2] ACCEPT.** Claim: \"The spike's \\\"slot 2 canary is GONE\\\" criterion can pass vacuously: if\n795: `readCanary(2)` throws after a successful reset (while the slot-1 read, which runs first,\n796: succeeded), the catch swallows the failure and `resetDone && slot2Canary === null` passes with\n797: the canary never read.\"\n798: Verified against db-pool.ts:1167-1194: the read order is exactly as claimed, and a slot-2 read\n799: failure after a good slot-1 read passes every criterion. Fix, as the reviewer proposes: the\n800: criteria gain \"the spike body completed without an exception\" (`failure === null`) and the\n801: non-empty before-snapshot criterion ([T12]).\n802: \n803: **[F3] ACCEPT — same defect as [T3], one fix.** Claim: \"`mirrorItemTree` copies the item tree's\n804: `supabase/` entire including CLI runtime state — `supabase/.temp` (whose `start-secrets/**` the\n805: .gitignore itself says \\\"must NEVER be committed\\\") and `supabase/.branches` — and deletes the\n806: slot's own copies.\"\n807: Ruled at [T3].\n808: \n809: **[F4] ACCEPT, as a named residual.** Claim: \"The only instruments that catch the incident's\n810: hybrid shape (slot ports + personal containers) are the `supabase_*` container-token scan and\n811: the personal-id substring check, and both depend on the CLI printing container names/project\n812: ids in status output.\"\n813: Correct, and not settleable today. Recorded in §6 as a residual pinned to the CLI version in\n814: devDependencies (`supabase ^2.110.0`): a CLI upgrade that changes status output shape must\n815: re-prove the identity-read instrument. [T2]'s docker corroboration narrows the exposure — the\n816: CLI's own output is no longer the only instrument on the destructive path.\n817: \n818: **[F5] ACCEPT.** Claim: \"`pathClosureProblems` cannot see multi-line array values: `scanConfig`\n819: reads one line per setting, so a valid multi-line `sql_paths = [\\n \\\"./x.sql\\\"\\n]` yields value\n820: `[` and no paths are extracted.\"\n821: For the OVERLAY an invisible value is copied verbatim — safe. For the CLOSURE an invisible\n822: value is a missed refusal — not safe. Fix: a PATH_KEYS entry whose value opens an array without\n823: closing it on the same line refuses loudly as unscannable, with the one-line rewrite named. A\n824: selftest carries the multi-line fixture.\n825: \n826: **[F6] ACCEPT.** Claim: \"`setup(
